// Plain words for this work, by passage key. See ./index.ts.

export const PLAIN_HERMETICA: Record<string, string> = {
  "herm.ch.1.1": "The narrator says that once, while he was thinking deeply about the things that exist, his mind was lifted up high and his bodily senses went quiet, like someone drowsy after a big meal or worn out. Then a being of boundless size seemed to call him by name and ask what he wanted to hear, see, learn and know.",
  "herm.ch.1.2": "The narrator asks, \"Who are you?\" The being answers that he is Man-Shepherd, the Mind of all power; he knows what the narrator wants and is with him everywhere.",
  "herm.ch.1.3": "The narrator says he wants to learn about the things that exist, understand their nature, and know God. The being tells him to keep everything he wants to know in his mind, and he will teach him.",
  "herm.ch.1.4": "As soon as he said this, the being's appearance changed, and in an instant everything opened up: the narrator saw an endless vision in which everything became a sweet, joyful Light, and he was carried away by it. Soon a frightening, gloomy Darkness settled over part of it, coiling like a snake. The Darkness turned into a kind of churning wet substance that gave off smoke as from a fire and let out an indescribable wailing groan, and then a wordless cry came from it, like a voice of fire.",
  "herm.ch.1.5": "Then a Holy Word (Logos) came down from the Light onto that wet substance. Pure, light, quick-moving Fire leapt up out of it to the heights, and Air followed the Fire, rising from the earth and water so that it seemed to hang from the Fire. Earth and water stayed so mixed together that no one could tell them apart, but they were stirred by the Word that ran through them.",
  "herm.ch.1.6": "Man-Shepherd asks the narrator if he understood the vision; he answers that he does not, but wants to know. Man-Shepherd explains that he himself is that Light, the narrator's God, Mind, which came before the wet substance that rose out of the Darkness; and the shining Word that came from Mind is the Son of God. He adds that what sees and hears in the narrator is the Lord's Word, while Mind is God the Father; they are not separate, and life comes from their union. The narrator thanks him, and Man-Shepherd tells him to understand the Light and make friends with it.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Nay; that shall I know\": \"No, but I shall come to know it.\"\n- \"Nay; that shall I know\": \"No; tell me, so that I may know it.\"\n- \"Not separate are they\": the Word and Mind are joined in the narrator himself, or in God.",
  "herm.ch.1.7": "Having said this, Man-Shepherd stared into the narrator's eyes for a long time, until he trembled. When he lifted his head, the narrator saw in Mind the Light, now made up of countless Powers, an ordered universe (cosmos) grown beyond all limits, and the Fire held in by an immense Power until it came to rest. He understood what he saw through Man-Shepherd's Word.",
  "herm.ch.1.8": "While the narrator was still amazed, Man-Shepherd told him that he had seen in Mind the original pattern, which exists before any beginning and has no end. The narrator asks where the elements of Nature come from, and the answer is: from God's Will. Nature received the Word, looked at the beautiful cosmos, and copied it, making herself into a cosmos out of her own elements and the births of souls.",
  "herm.ch.1.9": "God, who is Mind and both male and female, existing as Light and Life, produced a second Mind to shape things. This second Mind, a god of Fire and Spirit, made Seven Rulers who surround the world we perceive with our senses; their rule is what people call Fate.",
  "herm.ch.1.10": "At once God's Word leapt up out of the lower elements into the pure, shaped part of Nature and became one with the shaping Mind, since it was of the same substance. The lower elements of Nature were left without reason, as pure matter.",
  "herm.ch.1.11": "The shaping Mind, joined with the Word, surrounds the spheres and spins them, setting his creations turning from an unbounded beginning to an endless end, since their circling begins where it ends, as Mind wills. From the lower elements Nature produced creatures without reason, because the Word was not extended to them: the air produced winged things, the water swimming things, earth and water separated, and the earth brought out four-footed animals, reptiles, and wild and tame beasts.",
  "herm.ch.1.12": "Mind, the Father of all, being Life and Light, produced Man, equal to himself, and loved him as his own child, because Man was beautiful beyond compare and carried his Father's image. God truly fell in love with his own form and gave Man all his creations.",
  "herm.ch.1.13": "When Man saw what the shaping Mind had made in the Father, he wanted to make things too, and the Father allowed it. Moving into the sphere where things are shaped, where he was to have full authority, he looked at his Brother's creations; they fell in love with him and each gave him a share of its own ordering. Once he had learned their nature and shared in it, he wanted to break through the boundary of their spheres and overcome the power pressing on the Fire.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"They fell in love with him\": the Seven Rulers loved him, or all his Brother's creations did.\n- \"That which pressed upon the Fire\": the mighty Power that held the Fire in check, or whatever weighs down on the Fire from outside.",
  "herm.ch.1.14": "So Man, who had authority over all mortal things and unreasoning creatures in the world, leaned down through the framework of the spheres (the Harmony), broke through it, and showed God's beautiful form to Nature below. Seeing that endlessly lovely form, which held the powers of all seven Rulers as well as God's form, Nature smiled with love, as if she had seen Man's image reflected in her water and his shadow on her earth. Man, seeing a form like his own in her, loved it and wanted to live in it; as soon as he wanted it, it happened, and he brought that reasonless form to life. Nature then wrapped herself around him and they became one, like lovers.",
  "herm.ch.1.15": "That is why humans, unlike every other creature on earth, are double: mortal because of the body, but immortal because of the essential Man. Although deathless and with power over everything, a human suffers like a mortal and is subject to Fate. Though above the Harmony, he has become a slave inside it; though male-female like his Father, and sleepless like him, he is still overcome by sleep.",
  "herm.ch.1.16": "The narrator begs his Mind to keep teaching, because he too loves the Word. The Shepherd says this is the secret kept hidden until now. (A few words are missing here; a fragment mentions \"energy, and realization\".) Nature, embraced by Man, produced a marvel: because Man had the nature of the Seven, made of Fire and Spirit, Nature immediately gave birth to seven \"men\" matching the seven natures, male-female and moving through the air. The narrator, eager to hear more (the text has a gap here), begs him not to stop; the Shepherd tells him to be quiet, since he has not yet finished the first part of the teaching, and the narrator says he is silent.",
  "herm.ch.1.17": "That, the Shepherd says, is how those seven were born. Earth acted as the female, and her water was full of longing; she took ripeness from fire and breath from the upper air (aether), and so Nature produced bodies shaped like Man. Man changed from Life and Light into soul and mind: Life became soul, Light became mind. Everything in the world of the senses stayed this way until the end of that period and the start of new ones.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Her Water filled with longing\": Earth's water was full of desire, or water played the part of desire in the begetting.\n- \"Earth was as woman\": Earth was like a woman, or Earth served as the female element.",
  "herm.ch.1.18": "The Shepherd tells him to hear the rest. When the period ended, God's Will loosened the bond holding everything together. All living things, which had been male-female, were split apart along with humans, some becoming partly male and some partly female. God then spoke through his Holy Word: \"Grow and multiply, all you creatures and created things; and let the human who has Mind learn that he is deathless, and that the cause of death is love, though Love is everything.\"\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Love is all\": love is the whole of things, or love is present in all things.\n- \"Love is all\": love, though it causes death, is the greatest thing of all.\n- \"Partly male ... partly female\": each became wholly male or female, or only partly so.",
  "herm.ch.1.19": "After God said this, his Providence, working through Fate and the Harmony, brought about their pairings and births, and everything multiplied after its kind. Whoever has learned to know himself has reached the Good that lies beyond all plenty; but whoever, through a misguided love, spends his love on his body stays wandering in Darkness, suffering through his senses the things of death.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"That Good which doth transcend abundance\": a Good greater than any abundance.\n- \"That Good which doth transcend abundance\": an overflowing, more-than-abundant Good.",
  "herm.ch.1.20": "The narrator asks what great wrong the ignorant commit that they lose their deathlessness. The Shepherd says he seems not to have listened, and tells him to think: why do those who are in Death deserve death? The narrator answers that it is because gloomy Darkness is the root of the material body; from it came the wet substance, from that came the body in the sense world, and from that body Death drains the water.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Death dries the body out, drawing off the water it was made from.\n- Death feeds on the body's water, drinking it as its own.\n- Death draws its own power from that water, so the body is what feeds Death.",
  "herm.ch.1.21": "The Shepherd says he has thought rightly, and asks how \"the one who knows himself goes to God,\" as God's Word says. The narrator answers that the Father of all is made of Light and Life, and Man was born from him. The Shepherd agrees, and says that if the narrator learns that he himself is made of Life and Light, and that he happens to come from them, he will return to Life. The narrator then asks his Mind how he can come back to Life, since God says the one who has Mind should learn that he is deathless (the text breaks off briefly here).\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Thou happenest to be out of them\": you are made out of Light and Life.\n- \"Thou happenest to be out of them\": you are, for now, outside them and must return.",
  "herm.ch.1.22": "The narrator asks whether all people have Mind. The Shepherd says that he, Mind, is present with people who are holy, good, pure, merciful and devout. He helps them, and they quickly come to know all things, win the Father's love through their pure lives, and thank and praise him with hymns. Before they give up the body to its natural death, they turn away in disgust from its sensations, knowing what those sensations do; he, as gatekeeper, shuts the entrances and cuts off the thoughts that base, evil forces would stir up.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"From knowledge of what things they operate\": because they know what the senses bring about.\n- \"From knowledge of what things they operate\": they turn away also from the knowledge the senses produce.",
  "herm.ch.1.23": "But from those without Mind, the wicked, depraved, envious, greedy, murderous and godless, he stays far away. He leaves them to the Avenging Daimon (spirit), who stokes the fire in them, torments them, and rushes in through their senses, making them more ready to break the law and so suffer more. Such a person never stops craving excess, struggling endlessly in the dark.",
  "herm.ch.1.24": "The narrator thanks Mind for teaching him and asks to hear about the way up, as it is for him now. Man-Shepherd says that when the physical body is to be dissolved, a person first hands the body over to change, so its form disappears, and hands his way of life, now powerless, to the Daimon. The bodily senses go back to their sources, separate out and rise again as energies, while passion and desire return to the part of nature that has no reason.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"As now it is [for me]\": the way up as it lies before him now.\n- \"As now it is [for me]\": the way up as it now stands, in general.\n- \"Way of life\": his habits, or his character.",
  "herm.ch.1.25": "Then the person rises up through the Harmony, leaving something at each of seven zones. At the first he gives up the power of growing and shrinking; at the second, the scheming of evil; at the third, the deceit of desire; at the fourth, domineering arrogance; at the fifth, unholy boldness and reckless daring; at the sixth, the greedy pursuit of wealth by bad means; and at the seventh, deceiving falsehood, each now stripped of its power.",
  "herm.ch.1.26": "Stripped of everything the Harmony had put into him and clothed only in his own power, he reaches the nature of the Eighth sphere, where he praises the Father with those who are there. They welcome him gladly, and, having become like them, he also hears the Powers above the Eighth singing praise to God in their own language. Then they go up together to the Father, give themselves up to the Powers, become Powers themselves, and are in God. This, says the Shepherd, is the good end for those who have gained knowledge (gnosis): to become one with God. He asks why the narrator should wait: since he has received everything, should he not show the way to those who are worthy, so that through him humankind may be saved by God?",
  "herm.ch.1.27": "After saying this, Man-Shepherd merged with the Powers. The narrator thanked and blessed the Father of all and was released, filled with the power given to him and with what he had been taught about the nature of everything and the highest vision. He began preaching to people about the beauty of devotion and knowledge: \"You people born of earth, who have given yourselves to drunkenness, sleep and ignorance of God, sober up, stop overindulging, and stop being charmed by mindless sleep!\"",
  "herm.ch.1.28": "When they heard him, they all gathered. He asked them why, being earth-born, they had handed themselves over to Death when they could share in deathlessness. He urged those who walk hand in hand with Error and share their table with Ignorance to change their minds, leave the dark light, take part in deathlessness, and turn away from destruction.",
  "herm.ch.1.29": "Some of them joked and walked away, giving themselves to the way of Death; others begged to be taught and threw themselves at his feet. He lifted them up and became their guide home, teaching them how they would be saved; he planted words of wisdom in them and gave them deathless water to drink. When evening came and the sun began to set, he told them all to give thanks to God, and when they had finished, each went home to rest.",
  "herm.ch.1.30": "The narrator stored Man-Shepherd's kindness in his heart and, with all his hopes fulfilled, was overjoyed. The sleep of his body became the waking of his soul, closing his eyes became true sight, his silence was full of good, and his words gave birth to good things. All this came to him from his Mind, Man-Shepherd, the Word of all power, through whom, inspired by God, he reached the Plain of Truth; so he gives thanks to God the Father with all his soul and strength.",
  "herm.ch.1.31": "The narrator prays: Holy are you, God, Father of all; holy are you, whose Will is carried out by your own Powers; holy are you, who want to be known and are known by your own people. Holy are you, who made all things exist through the Word; holy are you, of whom all Nature is an image, and whose form Nature never made. Holy are you, stronger than all power, greater than all greatness, better than all praise. He asks God, who cannot be put into words and whose name only Silence can express, to accept the pure offerings of his reason, from a soul and heart always reaching up to him.",
  "herm.ch.1.32": "He asks God to hear his prayer that he may never lose knowledge (gnosis), which is the nature of our shared being, and to fill him with power and grace, so he can bring the Light to those of his race who are still in ignorance, his brothers and God's sons. For this reason he believes and bears witness, and goes to Life and Light. He blesses the Father and says that God's Man wants to be holy as God is holy, just as God gave him full authority to be.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Those in ignorance of the Race\": those of the human race who are still in ignorance.\n- \"Those in ignorance of the Race\": those who do not know the Race they belong to.",
  "herm.ch.2.1": "Hermes asks Asclepius whether everything that moves is moved in something and by something; Asclepius agrees. Hermes asks whether the space in which a thing moves must be bigger than the thing, whether the mover must be stronger than what it moves, and whether the nature of the space must be quite different from the nature of what moves in it. Asclepius agrees to each.",
  "herm.ch.2.2": "Hermes asks whether the cosmos is so vast that no body is bigger, and so massive that it is packed with all the other great bodies there are. Asclepius agrees. Hermes asks whether the cosmos is a body, and Asclepius says it is; then he asks whether it is a body that moves.",
  "herm.ch.2.3": "Asclepius agrees. Hermes asks how big, and of what kind, the space it moves in must be: must it not be far vaster than the cosmos, so that it has room to keep going and does not get cramped and stop? Asclepius, calling him Thrice-greatest, says it must be something immensely vast.",
  "herm.ch.2.4": "Hermes asks whether that space must be the opposite of a body, that is, bodiless, and Asclepius agrees. Hermes says space is therefore bodiless; and anything bodiless must be either something godlike or God himself. By \"godlike\" he means something that was never born, not something that comes into being.",
  "herm.ch.2.5": "Hermes says that if space is something godlike, it has substance; if it is God himself, it is beyond substance. But it should be thought of differently. God can be thought about by us, not by himself, because what is thought about falls under the thinker; God cannot be an object of thought to himself, since for himself he is nothing other than what he thinks. To us, though, he is something other, and so we can think about him.",
  "herm.ch.2.6": "So, Hermes says, if we think of space, we should think of it as space, not as God; and if we think of God, we should think of him not as space but as a power that can hold all space. Anything that moves moves in something still, and the mover must itself stay still, since it cannot move along with what it moves. Asclepius asks how things here can move along with things that are already moving, since Hermes said the wandering planets are moved by the fixed sphere. Hermes answers that they do not move with each other but against each other, and this opposition turns the resistance of their motion into rest.",
  "herm.ch.2.7": "So, Hermes says, the planets, moving opposite to the fixed sphere, are moved by each other through this opposition, and by the still sphere through opposition itself; it could not be otherwise. He asks whether the Bears (the constellations that never set or rise) are at rest or moving. Asclepius says they move, always turning around the same point. Hermes says that circling around the same point is held in place by rest: circling stops a thing from going off elsewhere, and so the movement is kept steady by its opposite.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"'Round-the-same' doth stop 'beyond-same'\": turning in a circle prevents motion away in a straight line.\n- \"'Round-the-same' doth stop 'beyond-same'\": the fixed centre holds back whatever would move past it.\n- \"The contrary stands firm\": the circling motion stays steady, or the rest opposed to it stays firm.",
  "herm.ch.2.8": "Hermes gives an everyday example: a person swimming. The water moves, but the push of his hands and feet keeps him steady, so he is neither swept away nor sunk. Asclepius says this is very clear. Hermes concludes that all motion happens in and through something still. So the motion of the cosmos, and of every living thing made of matter, is caused not by things outside it but by something inside it, such as soul, spirit or some other bodiless thing. It is not a body that moves the living thing.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Last sentence: a body does not move the living thing it contains.\n- Last sentence: not even the whole body of the universe can move a lesser body, even a lifeless one.\n- \"Things interior [outward] to the exterior\": things inside the cosmos, or things inside that reach outward.",
  "herm.ch.2.9": "Asclepius asks whether it isn't bodies that move logs, stones and other lifeless things. Hermes says no: what moves a lifeless thing is the something inside the body, and that is not a body, since it moves both the body doing the lifting and the thing lifted. A lifeless thing cannot move another lifeless thing; whatever moves something must be alive. So the soul carries a heavy load, lifting two bodies at once. And it is clear that moved things are moved both in something and by something.",
  "herm.ch.2.10": "Asclepius says that moving things must move in something empty. Hermes says no: nothing that exists is empty; only what does not exist is empty, and what exists can never turn into emptiness. Asclepius asks whether there are not empty casks, jars, cups and vats. Hermes laments how far he has strayed from the truth: does he think the fullest things are empty?",
  "herm.ch.2.11": "Asclepius asks what he means. Hermes points out that air is a body that runs through everything and fills it, and bodies are made from the four elements. So everything Asclepius calls empty is full of air, and so of the four elements. In reverse, the things he calls full are empty of air, because other bodies fill their space and leave no room for air. The things he calls empty should be called hollow, not empty, because they exist and are full of air and breath.",
  "herm.ch.2.12": "Asclepius agrees that air is a body that fills everything, and asks what to call the space in which the universe moves. Hermes says it is the Bodiless. Asked what that is, he says it is Mind and Reason (Logos): whole and complete in itself, holding everything, free of body and error, untouched by the senses, resting in itself and keeping all things in being; its rays, so to speak, are Goodness, Truth, a Light beyond light, and the original pattern of the soul. Asclepius then asks what God is.",
  "herm.ch.2.13": "Hermes says God is none of these things; he is what causes them all, and each individual thing, to exist. He has left nothing in existence that is not; everything comes from things that are, not from things that are not. Things that are not have no power to be anything, while things that are never have a nature of not being.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Nor hath He left a thing beside that is-not\": he has left nothing outside himself that does not exist.\n- \"Nor hath He left a thing beside that is-not\": he has not left anything over that is non-existent.",
  "herm.ch.2.14": "Asclepius asks what God is, then. Hermes says God is not Mind but the cause of Mind's existence; not Spirit but the cause of Spirit; not Light but the cause of Light. So God should be honoured with two names that belong to him alone: the Good and the Father. None of the other so-called gods, nor any human or daimon, can be Good in any way; only God is Good, and he is nothing but Good. Everything else is soul and body, which have no room to hold the Good.",
  "herm.ch.2.15": "Hermes says the greatness of the Good is as great as the existence of everything there is, bodily and bodiless, things sensed and things understood. So Asclepius should not call anything else Good, or he would be irreverent; nor should he ever call anything God except the Good alone, or he would again be irreverent.",
  "herm.ch.2.16": "Everyone talks about the Good, Hermes says, but not everyone understands what it is. People do not understand God either, and out of ignorance they call gods and some humans good, though these can never be or become Good, because they are very different from God, while the Good cannot be separated from God, who is the same as the Good. The other immortals are still honoured with the name of god, but God is Good by nature, not as a courtesy. The Good gives everything and takes nothing; God gives everything and takes nothing; so God is Good and Good is God.",
  "herm.ch.2.17": "Hermes says God's other name is Father, because he makes all things, and a father's role is to make. That is why, for right-thinking people, having children is a great and holy thing, and leaving life without a child is a great misfortune and an impiety; a childless man is punished by the daimons after death, his soul condemned to a body that is neither male nor female, a cursed thing under the sun. So Asclepius should not side with the childless man but pity his bad luck, knowing what awaits him. Hermes ends by saying all this should serve as an introduction to knowledge of the nature of all things.",
  "herm.ch.3.1": "God is the glory of all things, along with the divine and the divine nature. God is the source of all that exists: Mind and Nature, and Matter, the Wisdom that reveals everything. The divine is also a source: of Nature, Energy, Necessity, End and Renewal. In the deep there was boundless Darkness, Water, and a fine, intelligent Breath, all held in Chaos by God's Power. Then Holy Light arose, the elements gathered out of the wet substance under dry space, and all the gods sorted things out of fertile Nature.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Source [too] is Godhead, — yea Nature...\": the divine is the source of Nature, Energy, Necessity, End and Renewal.\n- \"Source [too] is Godhead, — yea Nature...\": the divine is itself Nature, Energy, Necessity, End and Renewal.\n- \"Yea Matter\": God is also Matter, or God is the source of Matter too.",
  "herm.ch.3.2": "While everything was still unformed and unmade, the light things were placed above and the heavy things were set down below, under the wet part of dry space; everything was bounded by Fire and held up, hanging in Breath. Heaven appeared in seven circles, its gods visible as stars with all their constellations, and Nature's parts were joined together along with the gods in her. Heaven's outer rim turned in a circle, carried along by God's Breath.",
  "herm.ch.3.3": "Each god, by his own power, produced what he had been given to produce. So there came four-footed beasts, creeping things, water creatures, winged things, every seed-bearing plant, grass and flowering shoot, all carrying the seed to be born again. The gods also brought forth human beings, to know God's works and bear witness to Nature's energy; to rule over everything under heaven and know its blessings; to grow and multiply; and, with every soul put into flesh by the turning of the circling gods, to observe the wonders of heaven, the works of God and the power of Nature, to know God's power and the fates that follow good and evil deeds, and to learn every good skill.",
  "herm.ch.3.4": "So people begin to live and grow wise, following the fate set by the circling gods, and they die for this end too. They will leave great memorials of their work on the earth, which leave faint traces when the cycles start again. Every living body, every fruit of seed and every human work, even if it decays, must be renewed, both by the gods' renewal and by the turning of Nature's wheel. For the divine is constantly renewing the mixture of the cosmos, and Nature herself is grounded in the divine.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Their deceasing for this end\": they die for this purpose, or their death too is set by that fate.\n- \"Godhead is Nature's ever-making-new-again the cosmic mixture\": the divine is the force by which Nature keeps renewing the cosmic mixture.",
  "herm.ch.4.1": "Hermes says the maker of the world made the whole world by Reason (Logos), not by hands, so we should think of him as everywhere and always existing, the one and only maker of all things, who created everything by his Will. His Body cannot be touched, seen or measured, and is like no other body; it is not fire, water, air or breath, yet all of these come from it. Being good, he wished to dedicate this Body to himself alone, and to put its earth in order and adorn it.",
  "herm.ch.4.2": "So he sent down to earth man, the ornament (cosmos) of this divine body: a life that cannot die and yet a life that dies. Man surpassed all other living things, and the world too, because of Reason and Mind. He became an observer of God's works, marvelled at them, and tried to know their maker.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"The Cosmos of this Frame Divine\": the adornment or ornament of this divine body.\n- \"The Cosmos of this Frame Divine\": an ordered world in small, made after this divine body.",
  "herm.ch.4.3": "Hermes tells Tat that God gave Reason to all people, but not Mind. This is not because he begrudges it, since envy does not come from God but lives down below, in the souls of people without Mind. Tat asks why God did not give everyone a share of Mind. Hermes says God wanted to set it up in the middle, as a prize for souls to win.",
  "herm.ch.4.4": "Tat asks where God set it up. Hermes says God filled a great Cup with Mind and sent it down with a Herald, who was told to announce to human hearts: \"Dip yourself in this Cup, if your heart can, if you trust that you can rise to the one who sent it, and if you know why you were born.\" Those who understood the message and immersed themselves in Mind shared in knowledge (gnosis) and became \"perfect men.\" Those who did not understand have only Reason, not Mind, and do not know why or by what they came to exist.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Cup\": a drinking cup, or a large mixing bowl.\n- \"Baptize thyself with this Cup's baptism\": plunge yourself into the cup, or undergo a rite of baptism.\n- \"What heart can do so\": if your heart is able, or every heart that is able.",
  "herm.ch.4.5": "Such people's senses are like those of animals; since they are made up entirely of feelings and impulses, they cannot appreciate what is truly worth contemplating. They focus all their thought on the body's pleasures and appetites, believing that is what humans exist for. But those who have received some of God's gift, Tat, have, judging by their deeds, been freed from the chains of death. Their Mind takes in everything on earth, in heaven, and anything beyond heaven; having risen that far they see the Good, and then regard their stay here as a misfortune. Looking down on all things, bodily and bodiless, they hurry towards the One and Only.",
  "herm.ch.4.6": "Hermes says this is the knowledge that comes from Mind, the vision of divine things, the knowledge of God, since the Cup is God's. Tat says he too wants to be dipped in it. Hermes says that unless he first hates his body, he cannot love his true Self; if he loves his Self he will have Mind, and with Mind he will share in knowledge. When Tat asks what he means, Hermes explains that one cannot give oneself to both perishing things and divine things. Existing things are of two kinds, bodily and bodiless, the perishing and the divine; whoever is free to choose must choose one or the other, since the two can never meet, and in such souls, as one shrinks the other grows.",
  "herm.ch.4.7": "Choosing the better not only gives the chooser the finest lot, making him a god, but also shows his reverence for God. Choosing the worse destroys the \"man\", but only disturbs God's order a little: just as processions pass through the middle of the road, doing nothing except taking the road from others, such people simply parade through the world, led by their bodies' pleasures.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Take the road from others\": block other people's way.\n- \"Take the road from others\": simply follow the road others have laid out.\n- \"Destroy the 'man'\": ruin the person himself, or ruin the true Man within him.",
  "herm.ch.4.8": "Hermes tells Tat that what comes from God has always been ours and always will be; but what depends on us should move forward without delay, because it is we, not God, who cause evil, by preferring it to good. He points out how many bodies we must pass through, how many groups of daimons, and how vast a system of star paths, to hurry to the One and Only God. The Good has no far shore, no limits and no end; in itself it has no beginning either, though to us it seems to have one: knowledge (gnosis).",
  "herm.ch.4.9": "So knowledge is not the Good's beginning; rather it gives us the first beginning of knowing it. Hermes urges that we take hold of that beginning and hurry through everything we must pass. It is very hard to leave the familiar things we see all around us and turn back to the ancient path. Things that can be seen please us, while unseen things are hard to believe in. Evils are the more visible things, but the Good can never show itself to the eyes, because it has no shape or form. So the Good is like nothing but itself, since something bodiless cannot make itself visible to a body.",
  "herm.ch.4.10": "Hermes explains how the \"like\" is higher than the \"unlike\": Oneness is the source and root of everything and is present in all things as their root and source. Nothing exists without it, while it comes from nothing but itself, since it is the source of everything else and can have no other source. Being the source, Oneness contains every number but is contained by none, and produces every number while being produced by none.",
  "herm.ch.4.11": "Everything that is produced is incomplete, divisible, and able to grow and shrink, but none of this is true of the Perfect One. What can grow grows from Oneness, but fails through its own weakness when it can no longer hold the One. Hermes tells Tat that he has now sketched God's image as far as it can be done. If Tat keeps his attention on it and looks at it with the eyes of his heart, he will find the path upward; the image will itself guide him, because the divine sight grips and draws in those who manage to open their eyes, as a magnet is said to draw iron.",
  "herm.ch.5.1": "Hermes tells Tat he will give him this teaching too, so that he is no longer without the mysteries of the God who is beyond every name; what most people think is hidden will become very clear to him. If God were visible, he would not truly be, because whatever becomes visible has come into being. The Unseen always is and has no wish to be shown; it always exists and makes everything else visible. God is not made; by thinking things into view he makes all things appear, and this \"thinking into view\" applies only to made things, since it is simply making.",
  "herm.ch.5.2": "So the only one who is not made is beyond being thought into view and is unseen. And because he makes all things visible, he shows himself through and in all things, and especially in whatever he chooses. Hermes tells Tat first to pray to the Lord and Father, the One and Only, from whom the One comes, to have mercy on him so that he can catch a single thought, a single ray, of this great God. Only thought can \"see\" the unseen, since thought itself is unseen. If Tat can do this, God will appear to the eyes of his mind; the Lord does not hold himself back but shows himself through the whole world. Tat can grasp the thought, see it, hold it in his own \"hands\" and look God's image in the face. But if even what is inside Tat is invisible to him, how could God, who is within him, be seen with outer eyes?",
  "herm.ch.5.3": "If Tat wants to \"see\" God, Hermes says, let him think of the sun, the moon's course and the order of the stars, and ask who watches over that order, since every order has limits set by place and number. The sun is the greatest of the gods in heaven, and all the others give way to him as king; yet this huge being, greater than earth and sea, puts up with smaller stars circling above him. Out of respect or fear of whom does he do this? Each star's path in the sky is different; who decides how and how far they move?",
  "herm.ch.5.4": "Hermes asks who owns the instrument of the Bear, which turns around itself and carries the whole cosmos with it; who set the sea its limits and put the earth in its place. There must be someone, he tells Tat, who is the maker and lord of all this; number, place and measure could not be kept without a maker. No order could come from something that has no place or measure; and even disorder has a lord, because it lacks something and so is under the one who has not yet put it in order.",
  "herm.ch.5.5": "Hermes wishes Tat could grow wings, fly up and, hanging between earth and heaven, see the solid earth, the flowing sea and its streams, the wide air, the quick fire, the paths of the stars, and the speed of heaven circling around them all. What a blessed sight, he says, to see all this under one rule: the unmoving in motion and the unseen made visible, which produces both the order of the cosmos and the cosmos we see as order.",
  "herm.ch.5.6": "If Tat wants to see God also through mortal things on land and in the sea, Hermes tells him to think of how a person is formed in the womb, look closely at the skill of the one who shapes him, and ask who makes this beautiful, godlike image. Who draws the circles of the eyes, bores the nostrils and ears, opens the mouth, stretches and ties the nerves, runs the veins, hardens the bones, covers the flesh with skin, separates fingers and joints, widens the soles of the feet, hollows out the ducts, spreads out the spleen, shapes the heart like a pyramid, sets the ribs together, widens the liver, makes the lungs like a sponge and the belly so stretchable, and puts the most honourable parts on show while hiding the least honourable?",
  "herm.ch.5.7": "Look, Hermes says, how many skills are used on one material and how much work on one sketch, all very beautiful, perfectly measured and yet all different. Who made them all? What mother or father, except God alone, unseen, who made everything by his Will?",
  "herm.ch.5.8": "No one says a statue or a painting comes about without a sculptor or a painter; so can such workmanship exist without a Worker? Hermes calls that deep blindness, irreverence and ignorance, and tells Tat never to separate the works from their Worker. God is greater than any name, the Father of all; he is the Only One, and his work is to be a father.",
  "herm.ch.5.9": "If Hermes may be bold enough to say it, God's very being is to conceive and make all things. Just as nothing can exist without its maker, God would not exist unless he were always making everything, in heaven, air, earth and the deep, in the whole cosmos, and in every part of all that is and is not. There is nothing in the world that is not God. He is both the things that are and the things that are not: the things that are he has made visible, and the things that are not he keeps within himself.",
  "herm.ch.5.10": "Hermes says God is beyond every name: unseen and yet most visible, contemplated by the mind and also seen by the eyes, bodiless and many-bodied, or rather the one in every body. There is nothing he is not; all things are him and he is all. So he has all names, since they all belong to one Father, and so he has no name of his own, since he is the Father of all. Hermes then turns to prayer: who can praise you, and where should he look to praise you, above, below, inside or outside? There is no direction or place around you; everything is in you and from you, who give everything and take nothing, since you have everything.",
  "herm.ch.5.11": "Hermes asks the Father when he should praise him, since no one can catch his hour or time; and for what, the things he has made or not made, shown or hidden? And how should he praise him: as belonging to himself, as having something of his own, or as being someone other? For God is whatever Hermes is, does and says; God is everything, and there is nothing he is not. He is what exists and what does not; Mind when he thinks, Father when he makes, God when he acts, and Good and maker of all. A bracketed note adds that the finest part of matter is air, of air the soul, of soul the mind, and of mind God.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"As being of myself?\": as someone who exists by himself, apart from God.\n- \"As being of myself?\": as one who is part of God, and so praises from within.\n- \"As being other?\": as someone separate from God, standing outside him.",
  "herm.ch.6.1": "Hermes tells Asclepius that the Good is found in no one but God; in fact God himself is always the Good. If so, the Good must be a pure being, free from all motion and change (though nothing is outside it), with a steady energy around itself, never too little or too much, always fully supplied. Though one, it is the source of everything, because what supplies everything is good, and always good, and this belongs only to God. God needs nothing, so he has no desire that would make him bad; he can lose nothing, so he feels no pain, which is part of badness. Nothing is above him to overpower him, no equal to wrong him or make him fall in love, nothing that ignores him to make him angry, and nothing wiser for him to envy.",
  "herm.ch.6.2": "Since none of these exist in God, what is left in him but the Good? Just as nothing bad is found in that supreme being, so the Good is found in nothing else, because everything else, small and great, each thing and even the cosmos, the greatest living being of all, contains all those other things. Things that are born are full of passions, since birth itself means being acted upon; and where there is passion there is no Good, just as where there is day there is no night. So the Good can never be in what comes into being, only in what is unborn. But because matter was given a share in everything, it also shares in the Good. In that way the cosmos is good: insofar as it makes all things, it is good, but in every other way it is not, since it is changeable, moving, and the maker of changeable things.",
  "herm.ch.6.3": "In humans, good is measured by having more or less bad: down here, what is not too bad counts as good, and good is just the smallest share of bad. So good here can never be clean of bad; it gets soiled, and once soiled it does not stay good but turns bad. Hence the Good is in God alone, or rather is God. Hermes tells Asclepius that people have only the name of good, never the thing itself, because no material body can hold it; the body is hemmed in by badness, toil, pain, desire, passion, error and foolish thinking. Worst of all, each of these is thought down here to be the greatest good; and worse still is greed of the belly, the error that leads all the other evils and turns us away from the Good.",
  "herm.ch.6.4": "Hermes thanks God for putting into his mind the knowledge that the Good can never be in the world, because the world is full of bad, while God is full of Good and the Good belongs to God. The qualities of the Beautiful surround the Good's very being; they seem so pure that perhaps they are its being. One might dare to say, if God has a being at all, that God's being is the Beautiful, and the Beautiful is also Good. No good can be got from things in the world: what the eye sees are images and pictures, while what it cannot see, especially the Beautiful and Good, is real. The eye can no more see the Beautiful and Good than it can see God, since they are part of God, belonging to him alone, his inseparable and most beloved companions.",
  "herm.ch.6.5": "If you can grasp God, Hermes says, you will grasp the Beautiful and Good, which go beyond light and are made brighter than light by God. That Beauty has no equal and that Good cannot be copied, just like God himself. Think of the Beautiful and Good as you think of God, since they can never be joined to any other living thing, being inseparable from God. If you are looking for God, you are looking for the Beautiful, and there is only one path to it: devotion together with knowledge (gnosis).",
  "herm.ch.6.6": "That is why people who do not know and do not follow the path of devotion dare to call a human beautiful and good, though he has never even dreamed of anything good. He is wrapped in every kind of badness, thinks the bad is good, uses it constantly, and even fears losing it, struggling hard not just to keep it but to increase it. Such, Hermes tells Asclepius, are the things people call good and beautiful, things we cannot run away from or hate; the hardest part is that we need them and cannot live without them.",
  "herm.ch.7.1": "The speaker cries out to people stumbling like drunks who have gulped the neat wine of ignorance and, unable to hold it, are already vomiting it up. He tells them to stop, sober up and look upward with the true eyes of the heart, or at least those who can. The flood of ignorance is pouring over the whole earth and swamping the soul shut up in the body, keeping it from reaching the harbour of salvation.",
  "herm.ch.7.2": "He urges them not to be swept away by the raging flood, but, those who can, to use the current along the shore to reach the harbour of salvation, and there to look for someone to take them by the hand and lead them to the gates of knowledge (gnosis). There the Light shines clear of all darkness, and no one is drunk; all are sober and look with their hearts' eyes on the one who wants to be seen. No ear can hear him, no eye see him, no tongue describe him, only mind and heart. But first you must tear off the cloak you are wearing: the web of ignorance, the base of badness, the chain of corruption, the shell of darkness, a living death, a corpse with senses, the tomb you carry around, the thief in your house, who hates you through what he loves and spites you through what he hates.",
  "herm.ch.7.3": "That is the hateful cloak you wear, he says; it chokes you and holds you down so that you cannot look up, see the beauty of the Truth and the Good in it, and come to hate the badness of the cloak. You would then discover its plot against you: it has made senseless the things people take for senses, blocking them with a mass of matter and stuffing them with disgusting lust, so that you cannot hear what you should hear or see what you should see.",
  "herm.ch.8.1": "Hermes tells his son they must now talk about soul and body: how the soul is deathless, and what drives the putting together and taking apart of the body. Nothing that exists actually dies; the word \"death\" either means nothing real, or is simply \"deathless\" with a syllable knocked off. Death means destruction, and nothing in the cosmos is destroyed. If the cosmos is a second god, a life that cannot die, no part of that immortal life can die; everything in the cosmos is part of it, and humans, the reasoning animals, most of all.",
  "herm.ch.8.2": "First of all comes God, maker of everything, eternal and beyond birth. Second comes the cosmos, made \"in his image,\" brought into being, sustained and fed by him, and made deathless by its Father, living forever. What lives forever differs from what is eternal: the eternal was not brought into being by another; and the ever-living, even if it was brought into being, was not brought into being by itself, but is always being brought into being. The eternal, being eternal, is the whole. The Father is eternal of himself, while the cosmos became eternal and immortal through the Father.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Even if He have been brought to being...\": this clause still refers to the Eternal, not to the cosmos.\n- \"He hath not been brought into being by Himself\": he did not come into being by his own act, or not alone, on his own.",
  "herm.ch.8.3": "From the matter laid up beneath him, the Father made one universal body, packed it together and made it a sphere wrapped around life, a sphere immortal in itself that makes matter last forever. Full of his ideas, the Father sowed living things into the sphere and shut them in like a cave, wanting to bring out life in every form. He surrounded the universal body with deathlessness so that matter would not want to break away from the body and fall back into its original disorder. For matter, Hermes tells his son, was disordered before it was given a body, and down here it still keeps some of that disorder around the small living things, in the growing and shrinking that people call death.",
  "herm.ch.8.4": "This disorder exists only around earthly creatures. The bodies of the heavenly beings keep the one order the Father gave them as their rule, and that order is kept unbroken by the restoration of each of them. For bodies on earth, being put together is their \"restoration,\" while their dissolving returns them to the bodies that can never be dissolved, that know no death. So what happens is a loss of the senses, not a loss of bodies.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Restoration of each one\": each heavenly body returning to its starting place in its cycle.\n- \"Restoration of each one\": each heavenly body being constantly renewed.",
  "herm.ch.8.5": "The third living being is man, made in the image of the cosmos and, by the Father's will, possessing mind beyond all earthly creatures. He not only senses the second god (the cosmos) but also has an idea of the first: he senses the cosmos as a body, and conceives of God as bodiless and the Good Mind. Tat asks whether this life does not perish. Hermes tells him to hush and understand what God is, what the cosmos is, what a deathless life is and what a life subject to dissolving is. The cosmos is through God and in God; man is through the cosmos and in the cosmos. God is the source, the limit and the structure of all things.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Doth have feeling with the second God\": shares feeling or sympathy with the cosmos, rather than just sensing it.\n- \"Beyond all earthly lives\": man has mind in a way no other earthly creature does, or man himself ranks above them.",
  "herm.ch.9.1": "Hermes tells Asclepius that yesterday he gave the Perfect Sermon, and today it is right to follow it by going through the Sermon about Sense point by point. Sense and thought seem to differ, since sense deals with matter and thought with true being; but to Hermes they seem to be one and the same, at least in humans. In other creatures sense is joined with nature, but in humans with thought. Mind differs from thought as much as God differs from divinity: divinity comes from God, and thought from mind. Thought is the sister of speech (logos); they are each other's tools, since speech cannot be spoken without thought and thought cannot be shown without speech.",
  "herm.ch.9.2": "So sense and thought flow together into a person, intertwined, since you cannot think without sensing or sense without thinking. Some say you can think without sense, as when people imagine sights in dreams. But Hermes thinks both activities happen in dreams, and sensing carries over from sleep to waking. A person is split into soul and body, and only when both sides of his sensing agree is the thought conceived by the mind expressed.",
  "herm.ch.9.3": "It is mind that conceives every thought: good thoughts when it receives seeds from God, and the opposite when it receives them from some daimon. No part of the cosmos is free of daimons, which creep in secretly to the daimon that is lit by God's Light and sow the seed of their own working in it. The mind conceives what was sown (the text seems to skip here): adultery, murder, killing one's father, sacrilege, irreverence, strangling, throwing people off cliffs, and all the other deeds of evil daimons.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"The daimon who's illumined by God's Light\": the human mind or soul, here called a daimon.\n- \"The daimon who's illumined by God's Light\": a good daimon, into which the evil one creeps.",
  "herm.ch.9.4": "God's seeds are few, Hermes says, but great, beautiful and good: virtue, self-control and devotion. Devotion is knowledge of God, and whoever knows God is full of good things and thinks godly thoughts, not the thoughts of the crowd. That is why those who have this knowledge do not please the crowd, nor the crowd them; they are thought mad and laughed at, hated and despised, and sometimes even put to death. Badness has to live here on earth, which is its proper place, not in the cosmos, as some irreverently say. But the devout person, once he has sensed this knowledge, will put up with everything: for him even things that are bad for others are good, because he relates them all to knowledge; and, most wonderful of all, he alone makes bad things good.",
  "herm.ch.9.5": "Returning to the teaching about sense, Hermes says that sense sharing with thought is what makes a human human. But not every person benefits from thought: one is material, another has true being. The material person, keeping company with badness, gets his seeds of thought from daimons, while people of true being, keeping company with the Good, are saved by God. God makes all things and in the end makes them all like himself; but they, while becoming good through their activity, produce nothing. It is the working of the cosmic cycle that makes them what they become, staining some with bad and cleaning others with good. The cosmos, too, Hermes tells Asclepius, has its own sense and thought, not like a human's, less varied, but better and simpler.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"They ... are unproductive things\": the things God makes, which become good but do not themselves create.\n- \"They ... are unproductive things\": people, who, though becoming good, cannot make things as God does.",
  "herm.ch.9.6": "The cosmos has a single sense-and-thought: to make all things and take them back into itself again, as the instrument of God's Will. It receives all the seeds from God, keeps them inside, brings them all into view, then dissolves them and makes them new again. Like a good gardener of life, it takes back what has been dissolved and renews it. There is nothing it does not give life to; taking all into itself, it makes them live, and is at once the place of life and its maker.",
  "herm.ch.9.7": "Bodies made of matter differ: some are of earth, some of water, some of air and some of fire. All are compounds, some more mixed and some simpler; the heavier ones are more compound and the lighter ones less. The speed of the cosmos's movement produces the many kinds of birth; being a very swift breath, it gives bodies their qualities along with the single fullness of Life.",
  "herm.ch.9.8": "So God is the father of the cosmos, and the cosmos is the father of everything in it. The cosmos is God's son, and the things in it exist through the cosmos. It is rightly called Cosmos, meaning Order, because it orders everything: the variety of births, leaving nothing without life, its tireless activity, its necessary speed, the mixing of its elements and the arrangement of its creatures. So it must and should be named Order. Other living things get their sense and thought from outside, breathed into them by what surrounds them, whereas the cosmos received them all at once when it came into being and keeps them as a gift from God.",
  "herm.ch.9.9": "But God is not beyond sense and thought, as some think; people say this out of superstition and irreverence. Everything that exists, Hermes tells Asclepius, is in God, made by God and dependent on him: things that act through bodies, things that move others through soul, things that give life through spirit, and things that take back what is worn out. Rather than saying God has these things, Hermes says the truth is that he is them all; he does not get them from outside but gives them out from himself. This is God's sense and thought: to keep everything moving. There will never be a time when even a scrap of what exists ceases, and a scrap of what exists means a scrap of God; God holds all that is, nothing exists without him, and he is not without anything.",
  "herm.ch.9.10": "Hermes tells Asclepius that if he understands these things they will seem true, and if not, unbelievable; to understand is to believe, and not to believe is not to understand. His words lead the way towards the truth, but the mind is powerful, and once words have led it so far, it can go ahead to the truth on its own. Having thought all this over and found it in agreement with what reason has already explained, the mind believes and rests in that good faith. So to those who, with God's help, understand what has been said, it is believable; to those who do not, it is not. That, he says, is enough on thought and sense.",
  "herm.ch.10.1": "Hermes says he gave yesterday's teaching to Asclepius, so it is only right to give today's to Tat, especially as it is a summary of the General Sermons addressed to Tat. \"God, Father and the Good,\" he tells Tat, have the same nature, or more exactly the same energy. \"Nature\" is a word for growth and is used of changing things, moving and unmoving, human and divine, each of which God wills into being. Energy is something different, as was shown in discussing other matters, divine and human, and this should be kept in mind when speaking about the Good.",
  "herm.ch.10.2": "God's energy is his Will, and his being is to will that all things exist. For what is \"God, Father and the Good\" but the existence of all that does not yet exist, the very ground of everything that is? That is God, Father and the Good, and nothing else is added to him. The cosmos, that is, the Sun, is also a father to those that share in him, but he is not the cause of good for living things, not even of their living. Even if he is a father, it is wholly because the Good's good will compels him, without which nothing could exist or come into being.",
  "herm.ch.10.3": "Likewise parents, mother and father alike, cause their children only by sharing in the Good's desire, which pours through the Sun; it is the Good that does the creating. Only he who takes nothing but wills all things to be can have this power; Hermes tells Tat he will not say \"makes.\" A maker falls short for long stretches, sometimes making and sometimes not, and varies in the quality and quantity of what he makes, sometimes producing a lot and sometimes the reverse. But \"God, Father and the Good\" is the cause of all things being; at least, so it is for those who can see.",
  "herm.ch.10.4": "The Good wills to be, and it is itself, above all, because of itself; everything else exists because of it, and the Good's distinctive feature is \"that it should be known.\" That, Hermes tells Tat, is the Good. Tat says his father has filled them so full of this good and beautiful vision that his mind's eye has almost become something to worship. Unlike the sun's rays, the vision of the Good does not blaze into the eyes and make them close; it shines out and increases the eye's sight, as far as a person can take in the brightness that only the mind can see. It reaches us more quickly, does no harm, and is full of immortal life.",
  "herm.ch.10.5": "Tat says those who can take in a little more of this vision than others often fall asleep out of the body into this most beautiful sight, as happened to their ancestors Uranus and Cronus, and he hopes the same will happen to them. Hermes agrees, but says they are not yet tuned to the vision, and do not yet have the power to open their mind's eye and gaze on the Beauty of the Good, a beauty nothing can spoil or grasp. Only when Tat can say nothing about it will he see it, because knowledge (gnosis) of the Good is holy silence and a rest for every sense.",
  "herm.ch.10.6": "Whoever perceives it can perceive nothing else, gaze at nothing else, hear nothing else or move his body in any way; stilling every sense and movement, he stays still. Then it shines all around his mind, shines through his whole soul, draws it out of the body and turns him entirely into true being. For, Hermes tells his son, a person's soul can be made like God even while still in a body, if it contemplates the Beauty of the Good.",
  "herm.ch.10.7": "Tat asks what \"made like God\" means. Hermes says every separate soul goes through changes, and asks whether Tat did not hear in the General Sermons that all the souls circling through the cosmos come from one Soul, the All-soul, as if divided off. These souls change in many ways, some to a happier state and some the opposite. Souls of creeping things become water creatures; water creatures become land animals; land animals become winged things; souls of the air become humans; and human souls take the first step to deathlessness by becoming daimons. So they circle up to the choir of the fixed gods (there are two choirs of gods, the fixed and the wandering), and this is the soul's most perfect glory.",
  "herm.ch.10.8": "But if a soul that enters a human body holds on to its vice, it neither tastes deathlessness nor shares in the Good; it hurries back down the path to creeping things. That is the sentence on a vicious soul. The soul's vice is ignorance: a soul that knows nothing of the things that exist, their nature, or the Good, is blinded and tossed about by the body's passions. This wretched soul, not knowing what she is, becomes a slave to strange, miserable bodies, carrying the body as a burden, ruled rather than ruling. Such ignorance is the soul's vice.",
  "herm.ch.10.9": "On the other hand, the soul's virtue is knowledge (gnosis): whoever knows is good and devout, and divine even while still on earth. Tat asks who such a person is. Hermes says it is someone who does not talk much or listen to much, because someone who spends his time arguing and hearing arguments is shadow-boxing; \"God, Father and the Good\" cannot be reached by speaking or hearing. Even so, all beings have senses, since they cannot exist without them. But knowledge is very different from sensing: sensing comes from what has power over us, while knowledge is the goal of understanding, and understanding is God's gift.",
  "herm.ch.10.10": "All understanding is bodiless, and its tool is the mind, just as the mind uses the body. So both come into bodies, things known only by mind and material things, because everything must be made of opposites; it cannot be otherwise. Tat asks who this material God is that Hermes speaks of. Hermes says the cosmos is beautiful but not good, because it is material and easily affected. Though it is first of all affected things, it ranks second in being and is lacking in itself. It was never born in time but always exists, yet its existence is a process of becoming, forever producing qualities and quantities, since it moves and is the source of all material motion.",
  "herm.ch.10.11": "It is a stillness known by the mind that moves material motion, because the cosmos is a sphere, that is, a head. Nothing of the head above is material, just as nothing of the feet below is mind; they are all material. The head itself, moving like a sphere, as a head should, is mind. Everything joined to the \"tissue\" of this \"head\", where the soul is, is by nature deathless, as things with more soul than body. Things further from this tissue, which have more body than soul, are by nature subject to death. But the whole is one living being, so the universe is made of both the material and what is known by the mind.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Naught of head above's material\": nothing in the upper, head part is material.\n- \"Naught of head above's material\": nothing above the head, beyond the sphere, is material.\n- \"Just as when body hath been made in soul\": like a body that has been formed inside a soul.",
  "herm.ch.10.12": "Hermes says the cosmos is the first living being, and man comes second, though first among things that die. Man has the same life-giving soul as all other creatures, yet he is not only not good but actually bad, because he is subject to death. The cosmos is not good either, since it moves, but it is not bad, because it does not die; man, subject to both motion and death, is bad.",
  "herm.ch.10.13": "A person's parts are carried one inside another: mind in reason, reason in soul, soul in spirit (breath), and spirit in body. The spirit runs through the body by the veins, arteries and blood, giving the creature movement and in a way carrying it. That is why some think the soul is blood; they mistake its nature, not knowing that at death the spirit first withdraws into the soul, then the blood clots, the veins and arteries empty, and the creature departs. That is the death of the body.",
  "herm.ch.10.14": "Everything hangs from one Source, and the Source hangs from the One and Only. The Source is moved so as to become a source again, while the One stands still forever and is never moved. So there are three: \"God, Father and the Good,\" the cosmos, and man. God holds the cosmos, and the cosmos holds man; the cosmos is always God's son, and man is, so to speak, the cosmos's child.",
  "herm.ch.10.15": "Yet God does not ignore man; he knows him well and wants to be known. Knowledge of God (gnosis) is a person's only salvation, the way up the mountain. Only through God does the soul become good, not good one moment and bad the next, but good of necessity. Tat asks what he means. Hermes tells him to look at a baby's soul, not yet cut off because its body is still small: it is wholly beautiful, not yet soiled by the body's passions and still almost hanging from the World-Soul. But when the body grows and drags the soul down into its bulk, the soul cuts itself off, brings forgetfulness on itself, and no longer shares in the Beautiful and Good; and that forgetfulness becomes vice.",
  "herm.ch.10.16": "Hermes says the same happens to those who leave the body. When the soul withdraws into itself, the spirit draws back into the blood and the soul into the spirit. Then the mind, stripped of its coverings and divine by nature, takes a fiery body and travels through all space, leaving the soul to its judgment and whatever punishment it deserves. Tat asks how the mind can be parted from the soul and the soul from the spirit, when Hermes had said the soul was the mind's garment and the spirit the soul's.",
  "herm.ch.10.17": "Hermes says a listener should think and breathe along with the speaker, and hear even more finely than the speaker's voice. This layering of garments happens only in a body made of earth. In such a body the mind cannot sit there by itself, bare: an earthy body could not hold so much immortality, and so great a power could not bear such close contact with a body that suffers. So the mind takes the soul as a kind of wrapping, and the soul, also divine, uses the spirit as its wrapping, while the spirit runs through the whole creature.",
  "herm.ch.10.18": "When the mind frees itself from the earthly body, it immediately puts on its own robe of fire, which it could not have while living in an earthly body. Earth cannot bear fire; even a small spark sets it ablaze, which is why water surrounds the earth as a guard and wall against the blaze. But mind, the swiftest of all divine thoughts and swifter than all the elements, has fire as its body. As a builder, mind uses fire as its tool for making things: the Mind of all for making everything, but the human mind only for things on earth. Without its fire, the mind on earth cannot make divine things, because its arrangement is human.",
  "herm.ch.10.19": "The soul in a human, not every soul but a devout one, is something divine, like a daimon. When such a soul is freed from the body, if it has fought the fight of devotion, which means knowing God and wronging no one, it becomes wholly mind. But the irreverent soul stays as it is, punished by itself, looking for an earthly body to enter, as long as it is a human one. No other body can hold a human soul, and it is not right for a human soul to fall into the body of an unreasoning creature; God's law protects the human soul from such an outrage.",
  "herm.ch.10.20": "Tat asks how a human soul is punished, then. Hermes asks what greater punishment there could be than irreverence: what fire burns so fiercely, what wild beast tears the body as irreverence tears the soul? He describes the torments of the irreverent soul, which shrieks: \"I'm burning, I'm on fire, I don't know what to say or do; I'm being eaten up by all the troubles around me; poor me, I can neither see nor hear!\" These are the cries of a punished soul, and it is not true, as most people think and Tat supposes, that a soul leaving the body turns into a beast. That is a serious mistake; the way a soul is punished is this (the explanation follows in the next section).",
  "herm.ch.10.21": "When mind becomes a daimon, the law requires it to take a fiery body to carry out God's services. Entering the most irreverent soul, it whips it with lashes made of its own sins. Then the irreverent soul, scourged by its sins, plunges into murder, outrage, blasphemy, violence of every kind and everything else that harms people. But the mind rides on the devout soul and guides it to the light of knowledge (gnosis); such a soul never tires of singing God's praises, blessing everyone, and doing good to all in word and deed, imitating its Father.",
  "herm.ch.10.22": "So, Hermes tells his son, Tat should praise God and pray to have the Good Mind as his mind. The soul passes to a better state, never to a worse one. Souls have dealings with one another: gods' souls with humans', and humans' with those of unreasoning creatures. The higher look after the lower: the gods look after humans, humans after animals, and God after everything, since he is above all and all are less than he. The cosmos is under God, man under the cosmos, and unreasoning creatures under man; God is over all and holds all. God's \"rays\", so to speak, are his energies; the cosmos's are natures; man's are the arts and sciences. The energies act through the cosmos and then, through its nature-rays, on man; the nature-rays act through the elements; and man acts through arts and sciences.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Have thy mind Good [Mind]\": that his own mind may be a good one.\n- \"Have thy mind Good [Mind]\": that the Good Mind (the Good Daimon) may become his mind.",
  "herm.ch.10.23": "This is how the universe is run: it hangs from the nature of the One and runs through everything by Mind, than which nothing is more divine or more powerful, and nothing better at uniting humans to gods and gods to humans. Mind is the Good Daimon. Blessed is the soul most full of him, and wretched the soul empty of Mind. Tat asks what he means. Hermes asks whether Tat thinks every soul has the Good Mind; he is speaking of that Mind, not the serving mind he just described, which is sent down to punish the soul.",
  "herm.ch.10.24": "A soul without mind \"can neither speak nor act.\" Often the mind leaves the soul, and then the soul neither sees nor understands but is like an unreasoning creature; that is how powerful mind is. But mind will not put up with a sluggish soul; it leaves it tied and bound tightly to the body. Such a soul does not have Mind, and such a person should not be called a man, because a human is a divine living being, measured not with the creatures on earth but with the beings in heaven called gods. Indeed, to speak boldly, the true \"man\" is even higher than the gods, or at least gods and humans are fully equal in power.",
  "herm.ch.10.25": "No god in heaven will come down to earth, crossing heaven's boundary, but man climbs up to heaven and measures it; he knows what in it is high and low and learns everything else exactly. Greater still, he rises above without even leaving earth, so wide is the reach of his rapture. So one may dare to say that man on earth is a mortal god, and a god in heaven is an immortal man. That is why everything is run through these two, the cosmos and man, but by the One.",
  "herm.ch.11.1": "Mind tells Thrice-greatest Hermes to master this teaching and remember what is said; since it has come to him to speak, he will not delay. Hermes says that since many people say many different things about the All and the Good, he has not learned the truth, and he asks his Master to make it clear, since he can trust an explanation of these things only from him.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Master this sermon\": take firm hold of this teaching.\n- \"Master this sermon?\": a question, \"Have you grasped this teaching?\"\n- \"As it hath come unto Me to speak\": since it has occurred to me to speak, or since it falls to me to speak.",
  "herm.ch.11.2": "Mind tells his son to hear how God and the All stand. There is God, Aeon (eternity), cosmos, time and becoming. God makes Aeon, Aeon makes the cosmos, the cosmos makes time, and time makes becoming. The essence of God is, as it were, the Good, the Beautiful, Wisdom and Blessedness; of Aeon, sameness; of the cosmos, order; of time, change; and of becoming, life and death. God's energies are mind and soul; Aeon's, lastingness and deathlessness; the cosmos's, restoration and its opposite; time's, increase and decrease; becoming's, quality. Aeon is in God, the cosmos in Aeon, time in the cosmos, and becoming in time. Aeon stands firm around God, the cosmos moves in Aeon, time is bounded in the cosmos, and becoming happens in time.",
  "herm.ch.11.3": "So God is the source of everything, Aeon their essence, and the cosmos their matter. God's power is Aeon, and Aeon's work is the cosmos, which never came into being once but is always coming into being through Aeon. So the cosmos will never be destroyed, because Aeon cannot be destroyed, and nothing in the cosmos perishes, because Aeon wraps it round on every side. Hermes asks what God's Wisdom is. Mind answers: the Good, the Beautiful, Blessedness and all Virtue; then, after a break in the text, something orders the cosmos, giving matter deathlessness and lastingness.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- What orders the cosmos is Aeon.\n- What orders the cosmos is God's Wisdom itself.",
  "herm.ch.11.4": "The cosmos's becoming depends on Aeon, as Aeon depends on God. Birth and time have two natures: in heaven they cannot change or be destroyed, but on earth they change and are destroyed. Aeon's soul is God, the cosmos's soul is Aeon, and earth's soul is heaven. God is in Mind, Mind in Soul, Soul in Matter, and all of them through Aeon. This whole Body, which contains all bodies, is full of Soul, Soul is full of Mind, and Mind of God. It fills the Body from inside and surrounds it from outside, making the All alive: outside, this vast perfect Life surrounds the cosmos; inside, it fills it with all living things; above in heaven it stays the same, and below on earth it changes as things come into being.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"It fills it from within\": God fills the Body.\n- \"It fills it from within\": Soul, full of Mind and God, fills the Body.\n- \"It fills it from within\": Aeon, this \"vast and perfect Life\", fills the Body.",
  "herm.ch.11.5": "Aeon keeps the cosmos going, whether by Necessity, Providence, Nature or whatever else anyone thinks; and all of it is God at work. God's energy is a power nothing can surpass, beyond comparison with anything human or divine. So, Mind tells Hermes, never think anything above or below is like God, or you will fall from the truth, since nothing is like what has no like and is alone and one. Never think anyone else could have his power: what besides him gives life, deathlessness and change? What else would he make? God is not idle, or everything would be idle, since everything is full of God. Nowhere in the cosmos or anywhere else is there idleness; \"idleness\" cannot apply either to the maker or to what is made.",
  "herm.ch.11.6": "Everything must be made, always being made, and according to the influence of each region. The maker is in all things, not settled in any one of them, not making one thing only but everything. Being power, he works in what he makes and is not apart from it, though what he makes is under him. Mind tells Hermes to look through him at the cosmos now before his eyes and study its beauty: a body perfectly pure, older than anything, yet always in its prime, always young, indeed growing ever more fully into its prime.",
  "herm.ch.11.7": "Mind tells Hermes to look at the seven worlds beneath, ordered by Aeon's order, filling Aeon with their varied paths. Everything is full of light, yet nowhere is there fire, because the love and blending of opposites and unlikes gives birth to light shining down through the energy of God, Father of all good, leader of all order and ruler of the seven world-orders. Look at the Moon, forerunner of them all, nature's instrument, changing the matter below; at the Earth set in the middle of everything, the foundation of the beautiful cosmos, feeder and nurse of earthly things. And see how vast is the crowd of deathless beings and of mortal ones, with the circling Moon midway between immortal and mortal.",
  "herm.ch.11.8": "All of them are full of soul and moved by it, each in its own way, some around heaven and some around earth; the right does not move to the left, nor the left to the right, nor the upper below, nor the lower above. Mind tells his dearest Hermes he no longer needs to be taught that all these come into being, since they are bodies, have souls and move. But they could not be brought together into one without someone to bring them together, and that someone must be wholly One.",
  "herm.ch.11.9": "Since their many motions differ and their bodies are unlike, yet one speed has been set for them all, there cannot be two or more makers. Among \"many\" makers a single order would not be kept; the weaker would compete with the stronger and they would fight. And if one maker made the changing, mortal beings and another the deathless ones, each would want to make the other kind too. And if there were two, with matter one and soul one, which of them would hand out the materials for making? If both had some, which would have the larger share?",
  "herm.ch.11.10": "Mind says to think of it this way: every living body, whether immortal, mortal or unreasoning, is made of soul and matter. All living bodies have soul, while things that do not live are matter alone. Likewise soul, in itself, is the cause of life after its maker; but the cause of all life is the one who makes the things that cannot die. Hermes asks how, then, mortal beings come to differ from deathless ones, and why the deathless Life that makes deathlessness does not make animals immortal.",
  "herm.ch.11.11": "Mind says it is clear, first, that someone does these things, and second, that he is One, since soul is one, life is one and matter is one. Hermes asks who he is. Mind answers: who else but the One God? Who else should put soul into living things but God alone? So God is one. It would be absurd, having admitted that the cosmos is one, the sun one, the moon one and the divine one, to want God himself to be one of a number.",
  "herm.ch.11.12": "So God makes everything, in many ways. And what is so great about God making life, soul, deathlessness and change, when Hermes himself does so many things? He sees, speaks, hears, smells, tastes, touches, walks, thinks and breathes, and it is not a different person doing each of these but one person doing them all. Yet none of these things could exist apart from God. Just as Hermes would no longer be alive if he stopped doing them, so God, if he stopped doing them (which it is not right to say), would no longer be God.",
  "herm.ch.11.13": "If it has been shown that nothing can be idle, how much less can God be? If there were anything he did not make (if one may say it), he would be incomplete. But since he is not idle but perfect, he makes everything. Mind asks Hermes to give himself to him for a little while, and he will understand more easily that God's work is one, so that all things may exist, whether being made now, made in the past or yet to be made. This, his beloved, is Life, the Beautiful, the Good, God.",
  "herm.ch.11.14": "To understand this in practice, Mind tells Hermes to look at what happens when he wants to father a child, though this is not quite the same, since God takes no pleasure in it. God has no one to share in his work; working by himself, he is always at work, being himself what he does. If he separated himself from it, everything would collapse and die, since life would stop. If all things are living and life is one, then God is one; and if all things in heaven and on earth live, and the one life in them is made by God and is God, then everything is made by God. Life is the union of mind and soul, so death is not the destruction of what has been joined, but the loosening of their union.",
  "herm.ch.11.15": "Aeon is God's image, the cosmos is Aeon's, the sun is the cosmos's, and man is the sun's. People call change death, because the body is dissolved and life, once dissolved, withdraws into the unseen. But in this teaching, Mind tells Hermes, the cosmos too changes, since part of it passes into the unseen every day, yet it is never dissolved. These are the things that happen to the cosmos: turnings and hidings, where turning is a return and hiding is renewal.",
  "herm.ch.11.16": "The cosmos takes every form; it has no forms outside itself but changes them within itself. Since the cosmos takes every form, what must its maker be? He should not be formless; but if he takes every form he will be like the cosmos, and if he has a single form he will be less than the cosmos. What, then, should we say he is, so as not to throw the teaching into doubt? (Nothing the mind rightly grasps about God is doubtful.) He has one form, his own alone, which cannot be seen since it is bodiless, yet he shows all forms through bodies. Mind tells Hermes not to be surprised that there is a bodiless form.",
  "herm.ch.11.17": "The passage starts mid-thought: it is like the form of reason (logos), or mountain tops in paintings, which seem to stand out boldly but are actually quite smooth and flat. Mind then asks Hermes to consider something said more boldly but more truly: just as a human cannot live without life, so God cannot live without doing good. Moving all things and making them live is, as it were, God's life and motion.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"The form of reason (logos)\": the form of a thought, real though it has no body.\n- \"The form of reason (logos)\": the form of a spoken word or speech.",
  "herm.ch.11.18": "Some of what is said, Mind warns, has a special sense, and he asks Hermes to understand what follows that way. All things are in God, but not as lying in a place, because a place is a body and does not move, and things lying in it have no motion. Things lie one way in the bodiless and another way when they are made visible. Think of the one who contains them all, and think that nothing is more all-embracing, swifter or more powerful than the bodiless; it is the most all-embracing, swiftest and most powerful of all.",
  "herm.ch.11.19": "Mind tells Hermes to judge from himself: tell your soul to go to any country, and it will be there faster than you can say it. Tell it to go to the ocean, and it will be there at once, not as if travelling from place to place but simply being there. Tell it to rise to heaven, and it will need no wings; nothing will stop it, not the sun's fire, the upper air, the whirling heavens or the bodies of the other stars; cutting through them all, it will soar to the last body. And if you want to break through that too and look at what lies beyond, if there is anything beyond the cosmos, you may.",
  "herm.ch.11.20": "See what power and speed you have, Mind says to Hermes; if you can do all this, can God not? So know God as holding everything in himself as thoughts, the whole cosmos itself. Unless you make yourself like God, you cannot know him, because only like can know like. Grow to the size of the Greatness beyond all measure; leap free of every body; rise above all time; become Eternity, and you will know God. Believing nothing is impossible for you, think of yourself as deathless and able to know everything: every art, every science, every way of life. Become higher than every height and lower than every depth; gather into yourself the senses of all creatures, of fire and water, dry and wet; think of yourself as everywhere at once, on land, at sea, in the sky, unborn, in the womb, young, old, dead and beyond death. If you know all these at once, times, places, deeds, qualities and amounts, you can know God.",
  "herm.ch.11.21": "But if you lock your soul up in your body and belittle it, saying \"I know nothing, I can do nothing, I'm afraid of the sea, I can't climb the sky, I don't know who I was or who I will be,\" then what do you have to do with God? You can know nothing beautiful and good while you love your body and are bad. The greatest badness is not knowing God's Good; but being able to know it, wanting it and hoping for it is a straight road, the Good's own path, which leads there and is easy. If you just set foot on it, it will meet you everywhere and be seen everywhere, even where and when you least expect it: awake or asleep, sailing or travelling, by night or day, speaking or silent. For there is nothing that is not an image of the Good.",
  "herm.ch.11.22": "Hermes asks whether God is unseen. Mind says, \"Hush! Who is more visible than he? He made all things for this one reason, that through them all you may see him.\" This is God's goodness and virtue, that he is made visible through everything. Nothing is unseen, not even bodiless things: mind sees itself by thinking, and God is seen by making. Mind tells Thrice-greatest Hermes that this much has now been shown to him; if he thinks over everything else in the same way within himself, he will not go wrong.",
  "herm.ch.12.1": "Hermes tells Tat that Mind comes from God's very being, if God has a being at all, and only Mind knows exactly what that is. So Mind is not cut off from God's being but joined to it, as light is to the sun. In humans this Mind is God, which is why some humans are gods and their humanity is close to divinity. For the Good Daimon said: \"Gods are immortal humans, and humans are mortal gods.\"",
  "herm.ch.12.2": "In unreasoning creatures, Mind acts as their nature, because where there is soul there is mind, just as where there is life there is soul. But in those creatures the soul is life without mind. Mind works in human souls for their good: in animals it cooperates with each one's nature, but in human souls it works against them. Every soul, as soon as it takes a body, is corrupted by pleasure and pain, which boil like juices in a body made of parts, and the soul is plunged into them when it enters.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"He counteracteth them\": Mind works against the natural leanings of human souls.\n- \"He counteracteth them\": Mind works against pleasure and pain in them.",
  "herm.ch.12.3": "Over whatever souls Mind rules, it shows its own light by working against their prior leanings, as a good doctor causes pain to a sick body, burning or cutting it to make it healthy. In the same way, Mind causes the soul pain to rescue it from pleasure, the source of all its ills. The soul's great illness is godlessness, followed by a liking for every evil thing and nothing good. So Mind, working against this, does good to the soul, as a doctor brings health to the body.",
  "herm.ch.12.4": "Human souls that do not have Mind as their pilot share the fate of animal souls. Mind works alongside them, giving free rein to the desires that carry them along, desires that, from the rush of lust, strain towards the unreasoning; so such human souls, like animals, never stop raging and lusting senselessly and never have enough of evil. Passions and unreasoning desires are very great evils, and over them God has set Mind as judge and executioner.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The one that \"becomes co-worker with them\" is Mind, which then goes along with their desires.\n- The co-worker is their own unreasoning nature, as in animals, not Mind.",
  "herm.ch.12.5": "Tat says that in that case the teaching about Fate that Hermes explained earlier is in danger of collapsing: if a man is absolutely fated to commit adultery, sacrilege or some other evil, why is he punished, when he did it out of Fate's necessity? Hermes answers that all deeds are Fate's, and nothing bodily, good or bad, happens without Fate. But it is also fated that the wrongdoer will suffer; he does the deed so that he may suffer for having done it.",
  "herm.ch.12.6": "Hermes tells Tat to set aside for now the teaching about vice and Fate, since they have discussed it in other sermons. The subject now is Mind: what it can do, how it differs, being one thing in humans and changed in animals, and how in animals it does not do good, while in humans it puts out the angry and lustful parts. Among humans, some must be counted as led by reason and others as unreasoning.",
  "herm.ch.12.7": "All people are subject to Fate, birth and change, which are Fate's beginning and end. And though all suffer what is fated, those led by reason (whom Mind guides) do not suffer in the same way as others; having freed themselves from vice, they are not bad, and so do not suffer bad. Tat asks whether the adulterer and the murderer are not bad. Hermes says that is not what he meant: the Mind-led man, though not an adulterer, will suffer as if he had committed adultery, and though not a murderer, as if he had committed murder. He can no more escape the nature of change than of birth; but someone who has Mind can free himself from vice.",
  "herm.ch.12.8": "That is why, Hermes tells his son, he has always heard the Good Daimon say (and had he written it down he would have helped humankind greatly, for he alone, as the First-born God who sees all things, truly speaks divine words) that \"all things are one, especially the bodies perceived only by the mind. Our life comes from God's Energy, Power and Aeon. His Mind is good, and so is his Soul. And since this is so, things known by the mind know no separation. So Mind, being ruler of all things and the Soul of God, can do whatever it wants.\"",
  "herm.ch.12.9": "Hermes tells Tat to understand this and apply it to his earlier question about Mind and Fate. If he carefully clears away all quibbling arguments, he will find that Mind, the Soul of God, truly rules over everything, over Fate, Law and all else; nothing is impossible for it, whether to set a human soul above Fate, or to place under Fate a soul that ignores what happens. That is enough, he says, from the Good Daimon's excellent words. Tat calls them divinely spoken, true and helpful, and asks him to explain something further.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"About Mind's Fate\": about how Mind relates to Fate.\n- \"About Mind's Fate\": about whether Mind itself is subject to Fate.\n- \"Neglectful of what comes to pass\": a soul careless of what happens, or careless of what will follow from its deeds.",
  "herm.ch.12.10": "Tat recalls that Hermes said Mind works in unreasoning creatures as their nature, cooperating with their impulses. But he thinks the impulses of animals are passions. So if Mind works along with those impulses, and they are passions, then Mind is a passion too, taking on the colour of the passions. Hermes praises the question as a noble one and says he should answer it nobly too.",
  "herm.ch.12.11": "Hermes says that all bodiless things, when in a body, are subject to being acted upon, and in the strict sense are themselves all \"passions\" (things undergone). Everything that moves something else is bodiless, and everything moved is a body. Bodiless things are in turn moved by Mind, and movement is a kind of undergoing. So both mover and moved are subject to it, the first ruling and the second ruled. When a person frees himself from the body, he is freed from passion too. But, more exactly, nothing is beyond being acted on; everything can be. Yet passion differs from being acted on: one is active, the other passive. Bodiless things act on themselves, whether they are still or moving, and either way it is a passion; bodies are always acted on, so they are passive. Hermes tells Tat not to be troubled by words: action and passion are the same thing, though there is no harm in using the nicer-sounding term.",
  "herm.ch.12.12": "Tat says Hermes has explained the teaching very clearly. Hermes adds that God has given humans two things beyond all other mortal creatures, mind and speech, both worth as much as immortality: mind to know God, and speech to praise him. Whoever uses them as he should will not differ at all from the immortals; and when he leaves the body, the two will guide him to the choir of the gods and the blessed.",
  "herm.ch.12.13": "Tat asks whether other creatures do not use speech. Hermes says no, they use voice, which is very different: speech is shared by all humans, while voice differs in each kind of creature. Tat objects that human speech also differs from race to race. Hermes agrees, but says humanity is one, so speech is one too; it can be translated, and it is found to be the same in Egypt, Persia and Greece. Tat seems not to know the worth and greatness of Reason (Logos), for the blessed god, the Good Daimon, has said: \"Soul is in Body, Mind in Soul, Reason in Mind, and Mind in God; and God is the Father of all these.\"",
  "herm.ch.12.14": "So Reason is the image of Mind, and Mind of God; Body is the image of Form, and Form of Soul. The finest part of matter is air; of air, soul; of soul, mind; and of mind, God. God surrounds and runs through everything, while mind surrounds soul, soul air, and air matter. Necessity, Providence and Nature are the tools that order the cosmos and matter; among things known by mind, each is a being, and their being is sameness. But each of the bodies of the cosmos is many: through having sameness, these compound bodies, though they turn into one another, still keep their sameness incorrupt.",
  "herm.ch.12.15": "In all other compound bodies there is a fixed number of each, since without number there can be no structure, combination or breaking apart. Units give birth to number and increase it, and when it breaks down they take it back into themselves. Matter is one, and this whole cosmos, the great god and image of the greater One, united with him and keeping the Father's Will and Order, is full of life. Through the whole of Aeon, the Father's everlasting re-establishment, there is nothing in it, whole or part, that does not live. Nothing dead has ever been, is, or will be in this cosmos, because the Father willed it to have life as long as it exists; so it must be a god.",
  "herm.ch.12.16": "So how, Hermes asks his son, could there be dead things in the god who is the Father's image, in the fullness of life? Death is decay, and decay is destruction; how could any part of what knows no decay decay, or any part of that god be destroyed? Tat asks whether the living things in it, its parts, do not die. Hermes tells him to hush; he is misled by the usual word for what happens. They do not die but are dissolved as compound bodies; dissolution is not death but the loosening of a compound, which happens not to destroy it but to renew it. What is life's activity, if not motion? And what in the cosmos does not move? Nothing.",
  "herm.ch.12.17": "Tat asks whether even the earth does not seem motionless. Hermes says no: she is the only thing that, though moving very fast, is also stable. It would be laughable for the nurse of all to have no motion, when she produces and gives birth to everything, since nothing can produce without motion. To ask whether this fourth part (the element earth) is inert is ridiculous, because a body with no motion shows nothing but inertness.",
  "herm.ch.12.18": "So, Hermes tells his son, everything in the cosmos is moving, either to shrink or to grow. What keeps moving also lives, but what lives need not stay the same. The cosmos as a whole, being simultaneous, does not change, but all its parts do; yet none of it decays or is destroyed. It is the words used that confuse people: life is not birth but sensation, and death is not change but forgetting. That being so, all the things that every living thing is made of, matter, life, spirit, mind and soul, are immortal.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Being simultaneous\": existing as a whole all at once.\n- \"Being simultaneous\": holding all its parts together at the same time.",
  "herm.ch.12.19": "Whatever lives owes its immortality to Mind, and humans most of all, since they receive God and share his substance. God has dealings with this kind of life alone: through dreams at night and signs by day, through everything, birds, entrails, wind and trees, he tells people the future. That is why humans claim to know the past, the present and what is to come.",
  "herm.ch.12.20": "Hermes points out to his son that every other creature lives in one part of the cosmos, water creatures in water, land creatures on land, flying creatures in the air, while humans use all of them, earth, water, air and fire; they see the sky too and touch it with their senses. But God surrounds and runs through everything, because he is energy and power; and it is not hard at all to think of God.",
  "herm.ch.12.21": "If Tat also wants to contemplate God, Hermes says, let him look at the ordering of the cosmos and how orderly it is; at the Necessity of visible things and the Providence of things that have come and are coming to be; at how matter is completely full of life; at this great god in motion with all the good and noble beings, gods, daimons and humans. Tat says these are just energies. Hermes asks: if they are just energies, who drives them but God? Just as heaven, earth, water and air are parts of the cosmos, so God's parts are life, immortality, energy, spirit, necessity, providence, nature, soul and mind, and the lasting of all these, which is called Good. There is nothing that has come or is coming into being in which God is not.",
  "herm.ch.12.22": "Tat asks whether God is in matter, then. Hermes says matter is distinct from God so that we can give it the quality of space. But what is it except a lump if it is not made active? And if it is made active, by whom? The energies, they said, are parts of God. Who brings all living things to life, makes immortal things immortal, and makes changing things changeable? Whether we speak of matter, body or being, these too are God's energies: materiality is matter's energy, bodiliness is bodies' energy, and being is the energy of being; and this is God, the All.",
  "herm.ch.12.23": "In the All there is nothing that is not God. So neither size, place, quality, form nor time surrounds God, for he is All, and the All surrounds and runs through everything. Hermes tells his son to give his worship to this Reason (Logos). There is only one way to worship God: not to be bad.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Unto this Reason (Logos)\": worship in keeping with this teaching.\n- \"Unto this Reason (Logos)\": worship directed to the divine Reason itself.",
  "herm.ch.13.1": "Tat tells Hermes that in the General Sermons he spoke in very unclear riddles about the divine, and hid his meaning when he said no one could be saved before being reborn. When Tat came to him as a suppliant on the way up the mountain and longed to learn the teaching on Rebirth, the one thing he did not know, Hermes promised to give it to him \"when you have become a stranger to the world.\" So Tat has prepared himself and made his thinking a stranger to the world's illusion. He asks Hermes now to fill what is lacking in him with the teaching on Rebirth he promised, in words or in the secret way. He says he does not know from what material or womb Man is born, or from what seed.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"In Wending up the Mount\": while the two of them were climbing a mountain.\n- \"In Wending up the Mount\": during the discourse known as \"Wending up the Mount\".",
  "herm.ch.13.2": "Hermes answers that Wisdom understanding in silence is the material and womb from which Man is born, and the True Good is the seed. Tat, completely lost, asks who sows it; Hermes says it is God's Will. Tat asks what kind of being is born, since he has none of that being which goes beyond the senses in him; will the one born be different, a Son of God? Hermes says he is all in all, made up of all powers. Tat complains that he is speaking in riddles, not as a father to a son. Hermes says this Race is never taught; when God wills it, God restores the memory of it.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"This Race\": this kind of being, the reborn.\n- \"This Race\": this kind of knowledge or teaching.",
  "herm.ch.13.3": "Tat says these things sound impossible and forced, and he wants direct answers; is he a stranger to his father's family? He asks Hermes not to hold back but, since he is a true son, to explain how Rebirth happens. Hermes says all he can tell him is this: whenever he sees within himself the simple vision born of God's mercy, he has passed through himself into a body that can never die. He is no longer what he was before but is born in Mind. The way to do this is not taught, and it cannot be seen with the composite body Tat sees with. His old composite form has been taken apart; he is no longer touched, yet he can touch; he has size, yet he is now a stranger to it. Tat sees him with his eyes, but cannot understand what he is, however hard he strains body and sight.",
  "herm.ch.13.4": "Tat says Hermes has thrown him into a wild frenzy of mind, since he can no longer see himself. Hermes wishes Tat had passed right through himself, like people who dream while not asleep. Tat asks who brings about Rebirth. Hermes says the Son of God, the One Man, by God's Will.",
  "herm.ch.13.5": "Tat says Hermes has now left him completely dumbfounded. Cut off from the senses he had before (a few words are missing here), he now sees his father's greatness as the same as his outward form. Hermes says he is wrong even in this: the mortal form changes every day, turned by time to growing and shrinking, because it is not real.",
  "herm.ch.13.6": "Tat asks what is real, then. Hermes says it is what is never disturbed and cannot be defined; what has no colour or shape, does not turn, wears no garment and gives light; what only it can understand, does not change, and no body can hold. Tat says he is truly losing his reason; just when he expected his father to make him wise, he finds his mind's senses blocked. Hermes says that is how it is: how can the senses grasp something that rises like fire yet sinks like earth, is wet like water yet blows like air, is neither solid nor liquid, which nothing can bind or loosen, and which can be known only through its power and energy? Even then it takes a person who can perceive the way of birth in God.",
  "herm.ch.13.7": "Tat asks whether he is incapable of this. Hermes says, God forbid: withdraw into yourself and it will come; want it and it happens; put the body's senses out of work and your divinity will be born; clear out the brutish torments of matter. Tat asks if he has tormentors in him. Hermes says many, fearful and of many kinds. Tat says he does not know them. Hermes names them: first Ignorance, then Grief, Excess, Lust, Injustice, Greed, Error, Envy, Deceit, Anger, Recklessness and Malice. These are twelve, with many more under them, and creeping through the prison of the body they make the person inside suffer through his senses. But they leave, though not all at once, the one God has taken pity on, and that is how Rebirth happens. (The text then breaks off with a mention of Reason.)",
  "herm.ch.13.8": "Hermes tells his son to be still and keep solemn silence, so that the mercy flowing to them from God will not stop. From now on he should rejoice, because God's Powers are purifying him so that Reason can take shape in him. Knowledge of God has come, Hermes says, and when it comes, Ignorance is driven out. Knowledge of Joy has come, and when it comes, Grief runs off to those who make room for it. He calls on the Power that follows Joy, Self-control, a most sweet Power, and says they should welcome it gladly, since its arrival chases away Excess.",
  "herm.ch.13.9": "Fourth, Hermes calls on Continence, the Power against Lust. (A few words are missing here.) This step, he says, is the firm seat of Justice, which has driven out Injustice without judgment; they are made just by Injustice leaving. He calls the sixth Power, Sharing-with-all, against Greed. With Greed gone, he calls on Truth, and Error flees, and Truth is with them. See how the Good is complete when Truth arrives, for Envy has left; and the Good is joined to Truth, along with Life and Light. Now no torment of Darkness dares come near; they have all been beaten and have fled on whirring wings.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Without judgment\": without any trial or sentence being passed.\n- \"Without judgment\": without any struggle, at once.",
  "herm.ch.13.10": "Hermes tells his son he now knows how Rebirth works. When the Ten arrive and drive out the Twelve, the birth in understanding is complete, and by this birth we are made gods. Whoever, through God's mercy, gains this birth in God and leaves the body's senses behind knows that he is made of Light and Life, and is filled with bliss.",
  "herm.ch.13.11": "Tat says that, made steady by God, he no longer looks at things with his eyes' sight but with the energy Mind gives him through the Powers. He is in heaven, on earth, in water and air; in animals and plants; in the womb, before the womb and after it; he is everywhere. Then he asks Hermes how the twelve torments of Darkness are driven out by ten Powers.",
  "herm.ch.13.12": "Hermes says the dwelling they have just passed through is made from the circle of the types of life (the zodiac), which has twelve parts but one nature, a form that takes every shape. Because of human delusion there are divisions among them, but in action they are one; Recklessness cannot be parted from Anger, or even told apart from it. So, reasonably enough, they all leave at once, since they are driven out by no fewer than ten Powers, the Ten. For the Ten gives birth to souls, and Life and Light are united there, where the One comes from the Spirit. So, by reason, the One contains the Ten and the Ten the One.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"For man's delusion there are disunions in them\": they are made to seem separate in order to deceive people.\n- \"For man's delusion there are disunions in them\": people, being deluded, wrongly see them as separate.",
  "herm.ch.13.13": "Tat says he sees the All and sees himself in Mind. Hermes says this is Rebirth: no longer looking at things from the body's point of view, a thing spread out in three dimensions. (The text is broken here.) He then refers to this teaching on Rebirth, on which he did not comment, so that they would not be seen as slandering the All to the crowd, which God himself does not want.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"On which I did not comment\": which he did not write a commentary on.\n- \"On which I did not comment\": which he did not speak about openly.",
  "herm.ch.13.14": "Tat asks whether this body made up of the Powers ever dissolves. Hermes tells him to hush and not speak of impossible things, or he will sin and the eye of his mind will be put out. The natural body the senses perceive is far removed from this essential birth: the first must dissolve, the second never can; the first must die, the second death cannot touch. Doesn't Tat know he has been born a god, a son of the One, just as Hermes has?",
  "herm.ch.13.15": "Tat asks to hear the hymn of praise that Hermes said he heard when he was at the Eighth (the Ogdoad) of Powers. Hermes says it happened just as the Shepherd foretold when he reached the Eighth. He tells Tat he does well to hurry to \"strike his tent,\" since he has been made pure. The Shepherd, Mind of all power, passed on to him no more than has been written down, knowing well that Hermes could learn everything himself, hear what he wished and see all things. He left Hermes to make beautiful things, and so the Powers within him, as in all things, burst into song.",
  "herm.ch.13.16": "Tat says he wants to hear and longs to know these things. Hermes tells him to be still and hear the hymn of praise that keeps the soul in tune, the Hymn of Rebirth, which he would not have shared so readily if Tat had not reached the end of all; so it is not taught but kept hidden in silence. He instructs Tat to stand in an open place under the sky, facing the south wind, around sunset, and worship; and likewise at sunrise, facing the east wind. Then he tells him to be still.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Reached the end of all\": come to the end of the whole teaching.\n- \"Reached the end of all\": reached the final goal, being fully reborn.",
  "herm.ch.13.17": "Hermes begins the hymn: let every part of nature hear it. He calls on the earth to open, on every bolt of the deep to be drawn back, and on the trees to be still, for he is about to praise the Lord of creation, the All and the One. He tells the heavens to open and the winds to be still, and asks God's deathless sphere to receive his words. He will praise the one who founded everything, fixed the earth, hung up the heavens, ordered the ocean to give fresh water to inhabited and uninhabited lands alike for everyone's use, and made fire shine for gods and humans for every task. He calls on all to praise him together, the one high above the heavens and Lord of every nature, the eye of Mind, and asks him to accept the praise of his Powers.",
  "herm.ch.13.18": "Hermes calls on the Powers within him to sing to the One and All along with his will. Lit up by blessed knowledge (gnosis), and praising through it the Light only the mind can see, he rejoices in the joy of Mind. He calls on all his Powers to sing: Self-control, Justice praising the just, Sharing-all praising the All, Truth praising truth, the Good praising the Good; and he tells Life and Light that their praises flow from him to them. He thanks God the Father, the energy of all his Powers and the power of all his energies.",
  "herm.ch.13.19": "Hermes says God's Reason sings his praises through him, and asks God to take the All back into his Reason through him, as a spiritual offering. So the Powers within him cry out; they praise God, the All, and do his Will. The Will comes from God, and the All goes back to him. He asks God to receive this spiritual offering from all things, and to keep the All within them: Life to preserve it, Light to illuminate it, God to breathe spirit into it. God's Mind acts as shepherd to his Word, he says, addressing the Creator who gives the Spirit.",
  "herm.ch.13.20": "Hermes ends the hymn: God's Man cries out to him through fire, air, earth, water and spirit, through all his creatures. From God's Aeon he has found how to praise, and in God's Will, which he was seeking, he has found rest. Tat says that by his father's kindness he has seen this hymn sung and has placed it in his own cosmos too. Hermes tells him to say \"in the cosmos only the mind can see.\" Tat agrees, saying the hymn has given him the ability and his mind has been lit up by it; he too wants to send his own praise to God from his own mind.",
  "herm.ch.13.21": "Hermes tells him to do so carefully. Tat says he will speak what he sees in his mind. He addresses the parent of his rebirth as God: \"I, Tat, send you spiritual offerings. God and Father, you are the Lord, you are Mind; receive from me the offerings you want, for by your Will everything has been made complete.\" Hermes tells him to send an offering acceptable to God, the Father of all, but to add \"through the Word.\" Tat thanks his father for teaching him to sing such hymns.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Thou Parent of my Bringing into Birth\": Hermes, whom Tat addresses as if he were God.\n- \"Thou Parent of my Bringing into Birth\": God himself, the true parent of Tat's rebirth.",
  "herm.ch.13.22": "Hermes says he is glad that Tat has produced the good, undying fruits of Truth. Now that Tat has learned this from him, he should promise to keep silent about his virtue and tell no one how Rebirth was handed on to him, so that they will not be thought of as slanderers. They have both paid enough attention, he as speaker and Tat as listener. In Mind, Tat has come to know himself and their shared Father.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Keep silence on thy virtue\": say nothing of the power he has received.\n- \"Keep silence on thy virtue\": keep his new goodness to himself, not boasting of it.",
  "herm.ch.14.1": "The writer (Hermes) tells the reader that while he was away, his son Tat wanted to learn about the nature of things and would not let him put it off, as is natural for a younger son newly learning each point of the teaching. So he was made to tell Tat more, to make it easier to follow. He now wants to pick out the main points of what was said and write them briefly to the reader, explaining them in a more hidden way, as to someone older and well versed in Nature.",
  "herm.ch.14.2": "If all visible things have been and are being made, and made things are not made by themselves but by another; and if made things are many, indeed all visible things, all different and unlike; then there must be someone who makes them. He cannot himself be made, and he is older than the things that can be made. Made things are made by something other than themselves, and of all things that exist because they were made, nothing can be older than them all except what cannot be made.",
  "herm.ch.14.3": "So he is supreme, one and only, truly wise in all things, since nothing is older than him. He rules over the number, size and differences of the things being made, and over the continuing of their making. Things that can be made can be seen, but he cannot. He makes so that he cannot be seen; he is always making, and so can never be seen. This is the right way to understand him; and understanding him, one should marvel, and marvelling, count oneself blessed for having come to know one's Father.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"For this cause He maketh, that He may not be able to be seen\": his purpose in making is to remain unseen.\n- \"For this cause He maketh, that He may not be able to be seen\": his making is the reason he cannot be seen, since only made things are visible.",
  "herm.ch.14.4": "What is sweeter than one's own true Father? Who is he, and how can we come to know him? Is it not right to give him alone the name God, or Maker, or Father, or rather all three: God for his power, Maker for his energy, and Father for his goodness? His power is different from the things being made, while his energy lies in all things being made. So we should drop the wordiness and silly talk and understand these two, the made and the Maker, for there is nothing in between and no third thing.",
  "herm.ch.14.5": "So in everything you think and hear, remember these two, and think that they are everything, doubting nothing, whether above or below, divine, changing or hidden. Everything is these two, Maker and made, and one cannot exist without the other: there can be no Maker without the made, since each is one and the same thing. So one can no more be separated from the other than a thing from itself.",
  "herm.ch.14.6": "If the Maker is nothing but what makes, alone, simple and not compound, it must do this making to itself, so that its making is its own being made. And whatever is being made cannot be made by itself but must be made by another. Without the Maker, the made is neither made nor exists, since each loses its own nature without the other. So if all things are agreed to be two, what is being made and what makes, they are one in the union of these: the one that leads and the one that follows. The making God is the one that leads; whatever is being made is the one that follows.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Do this [making] to Itself\": the Maker makes itself, its making being its own coming to be.\n- \"To Which its Maker's making is 'its being made'\": for the made thing, the Maker's act of making simply is its being made.",
  "herm.ch.14.7": "Don't be wary of made things because of their variety, for fear of giving God a low status and little glory. His one glory is to make all things, and this making is, as it were, God's body. Nothing bad or base is thought of as coming from the Maker himself. Such things are side effects of the making process, like rust on brass and dirt on a body; the brass-smith does not make the rust, the parents do not make the dirt, and God does not make evil. It is staying long in the state of being made that makes things lose their bloom, as it were; and that is why God made change, as a way of cleaning up what comes into being.",
  "herm.ch.14.8": "If one and the same painter can make heaven, gods, earth, sea, people, all the animals, lifeless things and trees, is it impossible for God to make all things? What a monstrous lack of understanding and knowledge of God! People who think this are in the strangest position: they say they worship devoutly and praise God, yet by not crediting him with making everything, they do not know God, and on top of that they commit the worst irreverence, attributing to him passions, arrogance or weakness. For if he does not make all things, it is either out of arrogance or inability, and that is an irreverent thought.",
  "herm.ch.14.9": "God has only one \"passion\": the Good; and one who is good is neither arrogant nor weak. For this is God: the Good, which has all power to make everything. All that can be made is made by God, that is, by the Good who can make all things. And if you want to learn how he makes and how made things are made, you can.",
  "herm.ch.14.10": "Here is a beautiful and very fitting image: a farmer sowing seed in the ground, wheat here, barley there, other seeds elsewhere; the same man planting vines, apple trees and other trees. In just the same way God sows immortality in heaven, change on earth, and life and motion in the universe. These are not many but few and easy to count: four in all, and God himself and Becoming, in which all things exist.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The four are immortality, change, life and motion, with God and Becoming added to them.\n- God and Becoming are counted among the four.",
  "herm.ch.16.1": "The writer tells the King that he is sending him a great teaching, a summary and digest of all the rest. It is not written to please popular opinion, since much of it refutes that, and it may sometimes seem to contradict his other teachings. His master Hermes often said, alone with him and sometimes with Tat present, that readers of his books would think them very simple and clear, when in fact they are unclear, with their inner meaning hidden; and they will become even less clear later when the Greeks want to translate them into their own language, which will badly distort and obscure what has been written.",
  "herm.ch.16.2": "Kept in its native language, the teaching keeps the meaning of its words clear, because the very sound and power of the Egyptian names carry in themselves the working of what is said. So the writer asks the King, as far as he can (and he can do anything), to keep this teaching from being translated, so that such great mysteries will not reach the Greeks, and so that the arrogant, loose and showy speech of Greece will not drain the strength from the solemn, powerful, active speech of the Names. The Greeks, O King, have new-fangled words good only for argument, and Greek philosophy is just a noise of words. \"We do not use words,\" he says, \"but sounds full of action.\"",
  "herm.ch.16.3": "The writer begins with a prayer to God, Lord, Maker, Father and Encompasser of all things, who though All is One and though One is All, since the fullness of all things is one and in one, the second \"one\" not being another but both being one. He asks the King to hold on to this idea throughout the teaching. If anyone tries to separate what seems to be All, One and the Same from the One, he will be taking the word \"All\" to mean a crowd rather than fullness, which is impossible, because separating the All from the One destroys the All. All things must be one, if they really are one; and they are, and will never stop being one, so that the fullness is not destroyed.",
  "herm.ch.16.4": "Look, he says, at the many springs of water and fire bursting out in the middle of the earth: in one place you can see all three natures, fire, water and earth, hanging from one root. That is why the earth is thought to be the storehouse of all matter: it sends out from its plenty and receives in return substance from above. In this way the Craftsman, meaning the Sun, forever orders heaven and earth, pouring down essence and drawing matter up, drawing everything around and towards himself and giving everything from himself to all. His good energies reach not only through heaven and air but onto the earth, right down to the lowest depth and the abyss.",
  "herm.ch.16.6": "If there is a being that only the mind can grasp, the writer says, it is the Sun's substance, and his light would be its store. But where this substance comes from or flows from, only he knows. The text is broken here; what survives says that in place and nature he is close to himself, and that since we cannot see him, we can only understand him by guesswork.",
  "herm.ch.16.7": "But seeing him is not left to guesswork: his rays shine in great splendour all around the cosmos above and below. He is set in the middle, crowned with the cosmos, and like a good charioteer he drives the team of the cosmos safely and holds them close to himself so they do not bolt in disorder. The reins are life, soul, spirit, deathlessness and becoming. He lets it run not far from himself, or, to tell the truth, together with himself.",
  "herm.ch.16.8": "In this way he works all things. He gives the immortals lasting permanence, and with the upper half of his light, which he sends upward from his side that faces heaven, he nourishes the deathless parts of the cosmos. With the side that sends light downward and shines around the whole sphere of water, earth and air, he gives life and keeps the creatures in these lower parts of the cosmos moving back and forth through births and changes. (The passage trails off here.)",
  "herm.ch.16.9": "He changes them in a spiral, turning them into one another, kind into kind and species into species, with their exchanges kept in balance, as he does with the great bodies. For every body, its lasting consists in transformation. An immortal body does not dissolve, but a mortal one does; that is the difference between the deathless body and the mortal one.",
  "herm.ch.16.10": "Just as his light is continuous, so his power to give life is continuous, never ending in space or supply. Around him are many choirs of daimons, like armies of many kinds. Though they live among mortals, they are not far from the immortals; their region stretches from here to the realm of the gods. They watch over human affairs and carry out what the gods have ordered, through storms, whirlwinds and hurricanes, changes by fire and earthquakes, and through famines and wars, punishing irreverence, which is humanity's greatest offence against the gods.",
  "herm.ch.16.11": "The gods' duty is to give benefits, humans' duty is to worship, and daimons' duty is to punish. Everything else people do, whether through error, recklessness, the necessity they call Fate, or ignorance, is not punished among the gods; only irreverence is found guilty at their court.",
  "herm.ch.16.12": "The Sun preserves and nurses every kind of thing. Just as the world known by the mind embraces the world of the senses and fills it, swelling it with forms of every kind and shape, so the Sun swells everything in the cosmos, giving birth to all and strengthening them. When they grow tired or fail, he takes them back into his arms.",
  "herm.ch.16.13": "Under the Sun is ranged the choir of daimons, or rather choirs, since they are many and varied, arranged beneath the groups of stars, as many for each. Drawn up in ranks, they serve each of the stars. By nature, that is, in what they do (since a daimon's essence is activity), some are good and some bad, and some are mixed, both good and bad.",
  "herm.ch.16.14": "All of them have been given authority over things on earth, and they cause the many confused upheavals on earth, for cities and nations in general and for each person individually. They shape our souls to be like themselves and set them moving with them, taking hold of nerves, marrow, veins and arteries, the brain itself, and down into the heart.",
  "herm.ch.16.15": "When each of us is born and brought to life, the daimons who are on duty at that moment of birth, ranged under each of the stars, take hold of us. They change every moment, not staying the same but circling round again. Coming down through the body into the two parts of the soul, they set it spinning, each towards its own activity. But the reasoning part of the soul stands above the daimons' control, designed to receive God.",
  "herm.ch.16.16": "Those few who have a ray from God shining on their reasoning part through the Sun are not affected by the daimons, because no daimon or god has any power against a single ray of God. Everyone else is led and driven, soul and body, by the daimons, loving and hating what they do. Reason is not the love that is deceived and deceives. So the daimons run this whole earthly order, using our bodies as their tools, and Hermes called this order Heimarmene (Fate).\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"The reason (logos) is not the love...\": the reasoning part is not the kind of love that deceives and is deceived.\n- \"The reason (logos) is not the love...\": this account does not concern the love that deceives and is deceived.\n- \"Loving and hating the activities of these\": the people love and hate the daimons' workings, or the daimons' own loves and hates drive them.",
  "herm.ch.16.17": "The world known by the mind hangs from God, and the world of the senses from the world known by the mind. The Sun, through both worlds, pours out abundantly the stream of Good from God, that is, the work of making. Around the Sun are the eight spheres that hang from him: the sphere of the fixed stars, the six of the planets, and the one around the earth. From the spheres hang the daimons, and from them, humans. So everything hangs from God.",
  "herm.ch.16.18": "So God is the Father of all, the Sun is their craftsman, and the cosmos is the tool of his craftsmanship. The being known by the mind governs heaven; heaven governs the gods; and the daimons, ranked under the gods, govern humans. This is the army of gods and daimons. Through them God makes all things for himself. All of them are parts of God, and if they are all parts, God is all. So in making all things he makes himself, and he can never stop making, since he himself never stops. Just as God has no end and no beginning, so his making has no end and no beginning.",
  "herm.ch.17": "Asclepius tells the King that, if he thinks about it, even bodies have bodiless things. The King asks what they are. Asclepius asks whether the bodies seen in mirrors do not seem bodiless; the King agrees and says he thinks like a god. Asclepius adds other bodiless things, such as forms, which seem bodiless yet appear in bodies, of living and non-living things alike; the King agrees. So, Asclepius says, bodiless things are reflected in bodies and bodies in bodiless things, that is, the world of the senses in the world known by the mind, and the reverse. Therefore he urges the King to worship the images, since they too have their forms from the world known by the mind. The King then rises and says it is time to see to their guests' comfort, and they will continue their sacred conversation tomorrow.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"The images\": statues of the gods.\n- \"The images\": reflections and likenesses in general, like those in mirrors just discussed.",
  "herm.ch.18.1": "The speaker says that when musicians perform and their instruments are out of tune, the performance becomes laughable; if the instruments are too weak for the music, the audience laughs at the musician, though he has given his skill willingly and tirelessly, and they blame his weakness. But God, the natural Musician, never grows tired, whether composing the harmony of his heavenly songs or sending the rhythm of his melody right down to each instrument; with God there is no weariness.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"They blame the [artist's] weakness\": the audience wrongly blames the musician himself.\n- \"They blame the ... weakness\": the audience blames the weakness of the instruments.",
  "herm.ch.18.2": "So if a musician enters the highest contest of his art, and after the trumpeters have played the composer's phrase and the flute players the sweet melody, the music is completed with pipe and plectrum and something goes wrong, nobody blames the musician's inspiration. They give him the respect he deserves and blame the faulty instrument, which has got in the way of the finest performers, hampered the musician in playing his tune, and robbed the listeners of the song's sweetness.",
  "herm.ch.18.3": "In the same way, the speaker says, let no one in the audience irreverently blame our race for the weakness that comes with having a body. Let him know instead that God is tireless Spirit, always the same in his knowledge, never ceasing his joyful gifts, and giving the same blessings everywhere.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Our Race\" (printed \"Kace\"): the human race as a whole.\n- \"Our Race\": our own kind or people, the speaker's company.\n- The garbled word may hide another word altogether, so the object of blame is uncertain.",
  "herm.ch.18.4": "And if the Craftsman, a Pheidias (the famous sculptor), finds his work does not come out right because the material is not good enough to show his skill, though he has done all he can, we should not blame him. We should blame the weakness of the string, which, being too slack or too tight, spoils the rhythm of the harmony.",
  "herm.ch.18.5": "So when a mishap is caused by the instrument, no one blames the artist. In fact, the worse the instrument, the more the artist's reputation grows each time his hand strikes the right note, and the more the listeners love him, with no thought of blaming him. \"So we too, noble sirs,\" the speaker says, \"will retune our own lyre within, to match the Musician.\"",
  "herm.ch.18.6": "The speaker says he has seen an artist who could not play the lyre, but once trained for a noble theme, often used himself as the instrument and tuned his string through mysteries, so that the listeners were amazed at how he turned necessity into splendour. He recalls the well-known story of the harper who won the favour of the god in charge of music: during a contest a string broke, but the god's favour gave him a replacement and the prize of fame. By the god's foresight a grasshopper settled on his lyre and filled in the tune in place of the broken string; so the harper's distress ended and he won.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"By means of mysteries\": through sacred rites.\n- \"By means of mysteries\": through secret techniques of his art.",
  "herm.ch.18.7": "The speaker says he feels this is his own case. A moment ago he seemed to admit his weakness and played the weakling for a while; but now, by the strength of that Superior One, as if his song about the King had been completed by him, he seems to awaken his muse. The aim of this task, he explains, will be the glorious fame of kings, and the speech will gladly dwell on their victories. So, he says, let us hurry, since the singer wants it and has tuned his lyre for it; he will play more sweetly and sing more fittingly because his song has greater subjects.",
  "herm.ch.18.8": "Since the singer has tuned his lyre especially for kings, in the key of songs of praise, with royal praise as his goal, let him first lift himself to the highest King, the God of all things. Starting his song from above, he then comes down in second place to those who hold the sceptre in God's likeness, since kings themselves prefer the song to come down step by step from above, so that their hopes are led in proper order from where their victories are granted.",
  "herm.ch.18.9": "So let the singer begin with God, the greatest King of all, forever free from death, everlasting and holding all the power of everlastingness, the glorious and first Victor, from whom all victories come down to those who win them in turn.",
  "herm.ch.18.10": "The speech now hurries down to praise the kings, the guardians of common welfare and peace, whose rule was set at the highest point long ago by the supreme God. Prizes have been prepared for them even before their bravery in war, and trophies set up before the battle begins. They are destined not only to be kings but to be the best, and foreign lands tremble before they even move.",
  "herm.ch.18.11": "Now the speech hurries to join its end to its beginning, with praise of the Better One, and then to finish with the most divine kings who give us the great prize of peace. As it began with the Better One and the Power above, so let it end by turning back to him. Just as the Sun, nurse of all growing things, gathers the first fruits as soon as he rises, using his rays like hands to pluck the sweetest parts of plants, so we too, beginning from the Better One and receiving the stream of his wisdom into the garden of our souls, should send these blessings back above the heavens to their source, since he himself poured into us all their power to grow.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Above the heavens\": describes the garden of our souls, which lies above the heavens.\n- \"Above the heavens\": describes where we turn the stream, back up beyond the heavens.",
  "herm.ch.18.12": "It would be fitting to use ten thousand tongues and voices to send blessings back to the all-pure God, the Father of our souls; and though we cannot say what is worthy of him, being far from equal to the task, we will say what we can. Newborn babies cannot sing their father's glory as it should be sung, but they give the thanks they can and are forgiven for their weakness. Indeed, God's glory lies in this very fact, that he is greater than his children, and the beginning, middle and end of praise is to admit that the Father is infinitely powerful and knows no limit.",
  "herm.ch.18.13": "It is the same with the King. As if we were the King's children, we feel it is our natural duty to praise him, though we must ask pardon for falling short, even though our Father grants it before we ask. Just as a father will not turn away from newborn babies for being weak, but will be glad when they begin to recognise his love, so the knowledge of the All, which gives life to all and the power to bless God in return, will rejoice.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"The Gnosis of the all\": the knowledge that we have of the All.\n- \"The Gnosis of the all\": the one who knows all things, that is, God.\n- \"The King's case\": the earthly king, or God as King.",
  "herm.ch.18.14": "God, being good, holding forever within himself the limit of his own eternal fitness, deathless, and holding the share of an inheritance that cannot end, always flowing from his own energy, sends messages to this world urging us to the praise that brings us home. With him there is no division and no favouritism; all are one in thought, one in foreknowledge, with one Mind, their Father. One feeling works through them, their love for one another; it is Love himself who brings about the one harmony of all things.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"They are one in Thought\": all beings are one.\n- \"They are one in Thought\": the gods, or God and the kings, are one.",
  "herm.ch.18.15": "So let us sing God's praise, the speaker says; or rather, let us first come down to those who have received their sceptres from him. We should begin with our kings, practising on them to get used to songs of praise and to train ourselves in devoted service to the Better One. Our first practice of praise should begin with the king, so that we exercise both our devotion to God and our praise of kings.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- \"Begin from him\": begin with the king.\n- \"Begin from him\": begin with God, the Better One.",
  "herm.ch.18.16": "We should repay the kings, because they have spread the blessing of such great peace to us. It is the king's virtue, indeed his very name, that establishes peace. He is called King because he flattens the peaks of conflict with his smooth step and is master of the reason that makes peace. Since he has made himself the natural protector of a kingdom that is not his homeland, his very name has become a sign of peace; the king's title alone has often stopped an enemy at once. Even the king's statues are safe harbours for those most battered by storms; the mere sight of his likeness wins victory and assures all citizens freedom from harm and fear.",
};
